1
00:00:30,843 --> 00:00:36,848
"THE ADJUSTMENT BUREAU - 2011"

2
00:00:36,849 --> 00:00:47,859
Sub.Re-Edit.by.
:: npdv.indoheroes[at]gmail.com ::

3
00:00:51,690 --> 00:00:53,660
<i>Let's welcome
our favorite alumnus,</i>

4
00:00:53,730 --> 00:00:56,760
<i>and the next senator
from the state of New York,</i>

5
00:00:56,830 --> 00:00:59,700
<i>David Norris!</i>

6
00:01:00,040 --> 00:01:03,130
<i>Thank you. Thank you.</i>

7
00:01:03,210 --> 00:01:04,870
<i>Well, hi, there.</i>

8
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:06,530
<i>My name is David Norris,</i>

9
00:01:06,610 --> 00:01:09,770
<i>and I'd like to be the next senator
from the great state of New York.</i>

10
00:01:11,710 --> 00:01:13,310
Look at you.
What's your name?

11
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:14,820
I'm going to go through
Yonkers, door to door

12
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:16,910
and take the city that way.
We love you.

13
00:01:17,050 --> 00:01:18,680
<i>The recent endorsement
by the fire fighter's union</i>

14
00:01:18,750 --> 00:01:20,150
<i>is another boost to Norris</i>

15
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:21,920
<i>who already had
a six-point lead</i>

16
00:01:21,990 --> 00:01:23,480
<i>over his opponent,
Roger Linfield.</i>

17
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:27,430
Whenever I see you on
C- Span, and you stand,

18
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:29,900
I always think you're
about to go, "Chug!"

19
00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:32,460
Congressman, you certainly
started your career off,

20
00:01:32,530 --> 00:01:35,770
<i>eight years ago, with a bang
or should I say, a bust.</i>

21
00:01:54,390 --> 00:01:56,190
<i>For the last eight years,</i>

22
00:01:56,260 --> 00:02:00,820
<i>David Norris has represented
Brooklyn, New York City and America.</i>

23
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:03,060
This is my campaign
chairman, Charlie Traynor.

24
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:04,190
These are voters.

25
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:06,030
Latest polls have
you up 10 points.

26
00:02:06,170 --> 00:02:07,970
<i>We're 10 points up?</i>

27
00:02:08,070 --> 00:02:09,900
<i>This is not going to be
easy getting there.</i>

28
00:02:09,970 --> 00:02:11,400
<i>I have some
real opposition,</i>

29
00:02:11,470 --> 00:02:12,740
<i>and what they're
saying about me</i>

30
00:02:12,810 --> 00:02:14,800
<i>is that I'm too young
to hold this office.</i>

31
00:02:14,910 --> 00:02:16,400
Win big, but run
like you're 20 down.

32
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:17,600
Okay, you got it.

33
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:18,700
Thank you, Gary.

34
00:02:18,780 --> 00:02:20,540
<i>But that's okay because
these are the same people</i>

35
00:02:20,620 --> 00:02:22,740
<i>who say that young
people don't vote,</i>

36
00:02:22,820 --> 00:02:25,120
<i>young people don't
care about politics,</i>

37
00:02:25,190 --> 00:02:26,680
<i>but I'm here to tell you,</i>

38
00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:29,720
<i>your future is about
your choices, not theirs.</i>

39
00:02:29,790 --> 00:02:32,060
<i>So, today I'd like
to put them on notice</i>

40
00:02:32,130 --> 00:02:34,930
<i>because come November,
I want them to know</i>

41
00:02:35,030 --> 00:02:36,960
<i>that it was young
people like you,</i>

42
00:02:37,030 --> 00:02:39,020
<i>who kicked their asses.</i>

43
00:02:42,940 --> 00:02:44,800
David! David!

44
00:02:50,650 --> 00:02:51,840
<i>Thank you.</i>

45
00:02:53,380 --> 00:02:54,370
Charlie.

46
00:02:54,450 --> 00:02:55,640
What's wrong?

47
00:03:15,840 --> 00:03:16,830
Yeah?

48
00:03:16,910 --> 00:03:18,300
<i>Any ideas?</i>

49
00:03:19,710 --> 00:03:21,140
I'm working on it.

50
00:03:21,210 --> 00:03:22,940
<i>Keep me posted.</i>

51
00:03:23,010 --> 00:03:24,210
Yeah.

52
00:03:51,070 --> 00:03:52,130
<i>David Norris,</i>

53
00:03:52,210 --> 00:03:54,730
<i>until recently, he was
way ahead in the polls.</i>

54
00:03:54,810 --> 00:03:57,330
<i>Mary Matalin and
James Carville are here.</i>

55
00:03:57,410 --> 00:03:59,880
<i>Mary, this is a surprise.</i>

56
00:03:59,950 --> 00:04:01,940
<i>It is a surprise.
Such political promise.</i>

57
00:04:02,020 --> 00:04:04,610
<i>This compelling story, he grew up
in a rough neighborhood in Brooklyn.</i>

58
00:04:04,690 --> 00:04:06,680
<i>He overcame the loss
of his entire family,</i>

59
00:04:06,760 --> 00:04:08,550
<i>his mom and his brother
when he was 10,</i>

60
00:04:08,620 --> 00:04:11,110
<i>his father before he got to
high school, he got over that.</i>

61
00:04:11,190 --> 00:04:12,390
<i>He had such promise.</i>

62
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:15,090
<i>And he was the
youngest person</i>

63
00:04:15,160 --> 00:04:17,100
<i>ever elected to the
House of Representatives.</i>

64
00:04:17,170 --> 00:04:19,900
<i>Yeah, he was elected
when he was actually 24,</i>

65
00:04:19,970 --> 00:04:23,030
<i>but he gets in a bar room fight
the night that he's elected.</i>

66
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:24,500
<i>I like this, all right.</i>

67
00:04:24,570 --> 00:04:26,940
<i>Then you have this photo
coming out of the New York Post.</i>

68
00:04:27,010 --> 00:04:29,140
<i>I think the accretion
of this stuff</i>

69
00:04:29,210 --> 00:04:31,610
<i>was just too much
for the voters.</i>

70
00:04:31,710 --> 00:04:34,680
<i>It reeked of some
level of immaturity here,</i>

71
00:04:34,750 --> 00:04:35,940
<i>of impulsiveness.</i>

72
00:04:36,050 --> 00:04:39,210
<i>People want maturity,
they want adults in Congress.</i>

73
00:04:51,570 --> 00:04:53,500
Big night
for us, gentlemen.

74
00:04:54,170 --> 00:04:55,690
Everybody ready?

75
00:04:59,140 --> 00:05:00,840
You look exhausted.

76
00:05:00,910 --> 00:05:03,400
You should take a vacation
when all this is finished.

77
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:04,640
You've earned it.

78
00:05:04,710 --> 00:05:06,310
I'm not sure the
kind of tired I am

79
00:05:06,380 --> 00:05:08,110
can be fixed by a vacation.

80
00:05:08,580 --> 00:05:10,810
Everybody needs
a vacation,

81
00:05:10,890 --> 00:05:12,480
even us.

82
00:05:13,360 --> 00:05:15,550
All right, let's get
him back on track.

83
00:05:15,620 --> 00:05:17,150
<i>Mary, over
the past couple of weeks</i>

84
00:05:17,230 --> 00:05:19,850
<i>since the photo came out,
they thought people...</i>

85
00:05:19,930 --> 00:05:21,660
Why are you still
watching CNN?

86
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:23,960
<i>That it wasn't such a big
deal, but clearly it was.</i>

87
00:05:24,630 --> 00:05:26,660
They called this way
too early. Come on.

88
00:05:28,440 --> 00:05:29,770
Put on your pretty
little tie and let's go.

89
00:05:32,440 --> 00:05:34,410
Suffolk County numbers,

90
00:05:34,610 --> 00:05:38,070
Linfield, 415,120.
Norris...

91
00:05:39,610 --> 00:05:42,050
370,233.

92
00:05:43,450 --> 00:05:44,920
Wow.

93
00:05:46,720 --> 00:05:48,690
I really thought
we'd win Suffolk.

94
00:05:48,760 --> 00:05:51,060
Kings County
just came in, too.

95
00:05:53,400 --> 00:05:54,760
Well...

96
00:05:55,360 --> 00:05:57,350
I didn't win Brooklyn.

97
00:05:58,030 --> 00:05:59,690
It's going to
be a blow out.

98
00:05:59,770 --> 00:06:02,100
NBC has us up next.

99
00:06:05,810 --> 00:06:07,040
I'm going to go
work on my speech.

100
00:06:07,110 --> 00:06:08,230
David...

101
00:06:08,310 --> 00:06:10,370
David. Hey!

102
00:06:11,910 --> 00:06:14,910
I'm sorry I wasted
your time, Charlie.

103
00:06:18,350 --> 00:06:22,260
<i>NBC news is now calling the
election for Roger Linfield.</i>

104
00:06:22,320 --> 00:06:24,810
<i>After a shockingly poor
showing at both Suffolk County,</i>

105
00:06:24,890 --> 00:06:26,590
<i>and his home county, Kings,</i>

106
00:06:26,660 --> 00:06:30,930
<i>it now appears David Norris
will lose this election badly.</i>

107
00:06:39,310 --> 00:06:40,900
Hello?

108
00:06:41,840 --> 00:06:43,110
Hello?

109
00:06:43,380 --> 00:06:44,370
I grew
up in Red Hook,

110
00:06:44,450 --> 00:06:45,810
and I don't want
to surprise anybody,

111
00:06:45,880 --> 00:06:47,870
but I got into a few
fistfights along the way.

112
00:06:47,950 --> 00:06:49,180
It's not whether or not
you get knocked down,

113
00:06:49,250 --> 00:06:50,770
it's what you do
when you get back up.

114
00:06:50,850 --> 00:06:52,620
Ladies and gentlemen, I'm
here tonight to tell you

115
00:06:52,690 --> 00:06:54,780
I will get back up.

116
00:06:56,730 --> 00:06:57,890
But tonight is
not about me.

117
00:06:57,960 --> 00:06:59,190
It is about
coming together

118
00:06:59,260 --> 00:07:01,560
and welcoming
the newest senator

119
00:07:01,630 --> 00:07:03,690
for the great
state of New York,

120
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:08,100
Roger Linfield.

121
00:07:09,370 --> 00:07:11,170
Shit!

122
00:07:11,240 --> 00:07:13,230
Shit, shit, shit.

123
00:07:13,840 --> 00:07:15,070
Hello?

124
00:07:26,890 --> 00:07:28,860
It's the men's.
Yep.

125
00:07:32,130 --> 00:07:34,360
Sorry about that.
I didn't mean to eavesdrop.

126
00:07:34,430 --> 00:07:37,190
I just didn't know
what to do.

127
00:07:37,270 --> 00:07:38,630
I heard you come in
and say, "Hello,"

128
00:07:38,700 --> 00:07:40,360
and I probably should
have said, "Hi,"

129
00:07:40,440 --> 00:07:42,060
but then I thought
that would be weird

130
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:44,070
because it's the men's.

131
00:07:44,140 --> 00:07:47,370
Then you started
talking to yourself

132
00:07:47,440 --> 00:07:48,970
and it was obviously
very personal.

133
00:07:49,040 --> 00:07:50,980
So I was stuck
in no man's land,

134
00:07:51,350 --> 00:07:53,940
and then it all got to be
too much, so I came out.

135
00:07:54,020 --> 00:07:56,280
What are you doing in here?

136
00:07:57,850 --> 00:07:59,680
Just...

137
00:07:59,750 --> 00:08:01,720
I'm hiding from security.

138
00:08:02,420 --> 00:08:03,450
Why?

139
00:08:03,830 --> 00:08:06,160
I crashed
a wedding upstairs.

140
00:08:06,330 --> 00:08:07,890
People still do that?

141
00:08:09,200 --> 00:08:11,100
It was a dare.

142
00:08:11,270 --> 00:08:13,960
Who dared you
to crash a wedding?

143
00:08:15,900 --> 00:08:17,100
Me.

144
00:08:17,170 --> 00:08:18,160
Oh.

145
00:08:20,540 --> 00:08:22,480
I crashed a wedding once.
Did you?

146
00:08:22,540 --> 00:08:23,940
Yeah, in high school.

147
00:08:24,010 --> 00:08:25,380
Oh.

148
00:08:25,810 --> 00:08:27,710
But I got caught.
I spent the night in jail.

149
00:08:28,780 --> 00:08:31,340
I think that happened more
than once from what I heard.

150
00:08:31,990 --> 00:08:34,390
You're that guy running
for Senate, aren't you?

151
00:08:34,460 --> 00:08:36,250
Yeah, I am that guy.

152
00:08:36,320 --> 00:08:38,720
And judging from your
speech, you're not winning?

153
00:08:38,790 --> 00:08:40,230
No.

154
00:08:40,360 --> 00:08:42,420
Oh. That's too bad,
the other guy is such a tool.

155
00:08:43,300 --> 00:08:44,890
He is a tool.

156
00:08:44,970 --> 00:08:47,800
I wish I could have made that
clearer in the campaign ads.

157
00:08:47,940 --> 00:08:49,730
Personally, I think
mooning your friends

158
00:08:49,800 --> 00:08:52,300
at a college reunion
is no big deal.

159
00:08:52,410 --> 00:08:54,430
<i>The Post did not have
to run the photo.</i>

160
00:08:54,510 --> 00:08:56,640
At least
not a full page.

161
00:08:56,710 --> 00:08:57,870
God, no.

162
00:09:00,350 --> 00:09:03,040
It was my favorite moment
of your whole campaign.

163
00:09:03,120 --> 00:09:05,020
Really? I could have
used you on my team

164
00:09:05,090 --> 00:09:06,450
a couple of months ago.

165
00:09:06,520 --> 00:09:07,610
I could help poll test

166
00:09:07,690 --> 00:09:10,420
every word that comes out of
your mouth before you say it.

167
00:09:10,490 --> 00:09:11,820
Oh, you like politicians?

168
00:09:11,890 --> 00:09:13,990
I like it when they do
stuff I can relate to.

169
00:09:14,100 --> 00:09:15,190
Like pull their
pants down?

170
00:09:15,260 --> 00:09:16,290
I love that.

171
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:20,700
See, that kind of candidate wouldn't
even get elected to the student council.

172
00:09:20,770 --> 00:09:22,740
My guy would know
how to tie his own tie.

173
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:23,860
It's a clip-on.

174
00:09:23,940 --> 00:09:25,630
Oh, I wish.

175
00:09:25,710 --> 00:09:28,140
That would have been my other
favorite moment of your campaign.

176
00:09:30,880 --> 00:09:32,370
Do you still
have a chance?

177
00:09:32,450 --> 00:09:34,240
Is it over?

178
00:09:34,320 --> 00:09:36,010
He crushed me.

179
00:09:37,020 --> 00:09:38,540
Sorry.

180
00:09:38,620 --> 00:09:40,650
Well, losing has
its advantages.

181
00:09:40,760 --> 00:09:42,250
Like what?

182
00:09:42,360 --> 00:09:45,380
For one thing, as a politician,
you're never really alone

183
00:09:45,460 --> 00:09:48,660
unless you're asleep,
or in the bathroom usually.

184
00:09:49,130 --> 00:09:51,390
That gets old.

185
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:53,160
Really?

186
00:09:53,270 --> 00:09:55,760
Yeah, I guess I'm mostly
just looking forward

187
00:09:55,840 --> 00:09:58,270
to having some
time by myself.

188
00:10:01,440 --> 00:10:05,210
I don't buy it.
I think you love it.

189
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:09,610
Do I know you?

190
00:10:16,620 --> 00:10:18,490
I guess I could have
been more convincing.

191
00:10:18,560 --> 00:10:19,620
Okay.

192
00:10:19,690 --> 00:10:23,130
You don't have to worry about being
convincing till the next election.

193
00:10:23,200 --> 00:10:25,190
Are you a registered
New York voter?

194
00:10:25,270 --> 00:10:27,890
Do I sound like I am?

195
00:10:43,650 --> 00:10:45,350
Holy shit.

196
00:10:48,020 --> 00:10:49,180
David.

197
00:10:50,060 --> 00:10:52,150
Oh. Wow, sorry, guys.

198
00:10:53,330 --> 00:10:55,260
Sorry.
I got to go.

199
00:10:57,030 --> 00:10:58,500
Sorry.

200
00:11:00,470 --> 00:11:01,530
Wow.
Hang on.

201
00:11:01,600 --> 00:11:02,690
You going to give your speech?
Hang on.

202
00:11:03,440 --> 00:11:04,630
Hey!

203
00:11:05,240 --> 00:11:06,300
Hey.

204
00:11:08,510 --> 00:11:09,570
Ma'am.

205
00:11:09,640 --> 00:11:11,240
I got to go.

206
00:11:11,910 --> 00:11:12,900
Ma'am?

207
00:11:12,980 --> 00:11:14,310
That is so much better.

208
00:11:14,420 --> 00:11:15,710
She's on the move.

209
00:11:16,990 --> 00:11:18,480
Southwest staircase.

210
00:11:18,550 --> 00:11:21,150
Excuse me? Ma'am.

211
00:11:21,220 --> 00:11:23,050
What was that?

212
00:11:23,320 --> 00:11:25,160
I don't know.

213
00:11:25,360 --> 00:11:27,020
Are you ready?

214
00:11:27,100 --> 00:11:28,620
Yeah, yeah.

215
00:11:29,030 --> 00:11:31,460
All right, go do
the speech. Come on.

216
00:11:31,530 --> 00:11:34,430
<i>Congressman David Norris.</i>

217
00:11:38,070 --> 00:11:40,270
<i>I grew up not far away
from here, in Red Hook.</i>

218
00:11:40,340 --> 00:11:42,430
Brooklyn!
I love you, David!

219
00:11:42,510 --> 00:11:44,170
I love you more!

220
00:11:44,250 --> 00:11:45,640
<i>I love you both.</i>

221
00:11:46,950 --> 00:11:49,610
<i>But we had a rule
in my neighborhood.</i>

222
00:11:49,750 --> 00:11:52,310
<i>When you got in a fight,</i>

223
00:11:53,320 --> 00:11:56,450
<i>it wasn't whether or not
you got knocked down,</i>

224
00:11:56,520 --> 00:12:00,430
<i>it's what you do
when you get back up.</i>

225
00:12:04,270 --> 00:12:06,100
<i>And I came here
to tell you tonight,</i>

226
00:12:06,170 --> 00:12:09,000
<i>that I will get back up.</i>

227
00:12:25,720 --> 00:12:27,120
<i>It's bullshit.</i>

228
00:12:27,790 --> 00:12:30,420
<i>We didn't have that saying
in my neighborhood.</i>

229
00:12:31,130 --> 00:12:33,650
<i>It's just one
of those phrases</i>

230
00:12:35,130 --> 00:12:37,360
<i>that had some traction
with the focus group</i>

231
00:12:37,430 --> 00:12:40,400
<i>and so we kept using it,
but it's not true.</i>

232
00:12:40,470 --> 00:12:43,340
<i>In 1998, I did a
cover story for GQ.</i>

233
00:12:43,400 --> 00:12:46,500
<i>The title was
"Youngest Congressman Ever. "</i>

234
00:12:46,570 --> 00:12:48,410
<i>And since then,
every story has tried</i>

235
00:12:48,510 --> 00:12:51,380
<i>to explain how
I got here so fast.</i>

236
00:12:53,080 --> 00:12:57,580
<i>And the word that people
kept using was "authentic. "</i>

237
00:13:00,190 --> 00:13:03,210
<i>But here's the problem.</i>

238
00:13:05,390 --> 00:13:07,660
<i>This isn't even my tie.</i>

239
00:13:08,500 --> 00:13:10,520
<i>Go get Jim for me.
This tie was selected for me</i>

240
00:13:10,600 --> 00:13:14,690
<i>by a group of specialists
in Tenafly, New Jersey</i>

241
00:13:14,770 --> 00:13:18,710
<i>who chose it over
56 other ties we tested.</i>

242
00:13:18,940 --> 00:13:23,140
<i>In fact, our data suggests
that I have to stick to</i>

243
00:13:23,340 --> 00:13:27,080
<i>either a tie that is red
or a tie that is blue.</i>

244
00:13:27,180 --> 00:13:30,810
<i>A yellow tie made it look as if
I was taking my situation lightly,</i>

245
00:13:30,890 --> 00:13:33,450
<i>and I may in fact, pull my
pants down again at any moment.</i>

246
00:13:35,260 --> 00:13:38,560
<i>A silver tie meant that
I'd forgotten my roots.</i>

247
00:13:39,830 --> 00:13:41,760
<i>My shoes...</i>

248
