Hey, hey. God! Hey!

499
00:28:01,090 --> 00:28:03,490
What's with you guys?
Jesus!

500
00:28:06,590 --> 00:28:09,590
Oh, come on. No! No!

501
00:28:10,060 --> 00:28:11,790
What the hell?

502
00:28:13,030 --> 00:28:14,520
Really?

503
00:28:15,740 --> 00:28:17,760
What the hell?

504
00:28:18,810 --> 00:28:20,430
Okay.

505
00:28:23,080 --> 00:28:24,240
Okay.
Take him back.

506
00:28:24,310 --> 00:28:25,370
Enjoy the rest
of your day.

507
00:28:25,450 --> 00:28:27,880
I can walk.
I'm capable of walking.

508
00:28:40,460 --> 00:28:42,360
Dude, what are you
doing on my floor?

509
00:28:42,460 --> 00:28:45,230
You know, I called you like 10
times, you could have picked up.

510
00:28:49,100 --> 00:28:51,200
Christine, I found him.
He's in my office.

511
00:28:51,270 --> 00:28:52,600
What the hell
are you doing?

512
00:28:54,710 --> 00:28:57,470
Round up the guys, we're
going to do the meeting now.

513
00:28:58,710 --> 00:28:59,910
You have a headache?

514
00:29:00,450 --> 00:29:01,740
What is wrong with you?

515
00:29:05,250 --> 00:29:08,190
Hey, we're in the big
conference room on 17. Let's go.

516
00:29:16,830 --> 00:29:18,760
Morning, Congressman.

517
00:29:20,170 --> 00:29:21,460
You sure you're okay?

518
00:29:21,540 --> 00:29:23,200
Yeah, you?

519
00:29:23,270 --> 00:29:24,360
I'm great, why?

520
00:29:24,470 --> 00:29:26,100
You don't have
a headache or anything?

521
00:29:26,170 --> 00:29:28,000
No, I feel fantastic, man.

522
00:29:28,080 --> 00:29:30,600
Is it me or this
an odd conversation?

523
00:29:30,980 --> 00:29:32,410
On David's
solar panel deal,

524
00:29:32,480 --> 00:29:33,810
our tech guy has
serious doubts

525
00:29:33,950 --> 00:29:35,940
about the efficiency
ratings they're claiming.

526
00:29:36,020 --> 00:29:38,250
They'd need a 20-fold improvement
over anything anyone else

527
00:29:38,320 --> 00:29:41,810
has been able to manufacture at that
price point to really be a game-changer.

528
00:29:42,420 --> 00:29:45,120
You're opposed to this anyway,
Charlie. And with these financials...

529
00:29:45,190 --> 00:29:47,290
Whoa, whoa.
I know it's a risk,

530
00:29:47,360 --> 00:29:51,420
but if our company doesn't take a chance
on something like this, then who will?

531
00:29:51,570 --> 00:29:55,520
I mean, I'm willing to take a fire on
something that could change the world.

532
00:29:57,000 --> 00:29:59,800
Not to mention,
be incredibly lucrative.

533
00:30:05,380 --> 00:30:06,540
David?

534
00:30:09,550 --> 00:30:13,820
Yeah, I mean, it's why
I took the job here.

535
00:30:15,390 --> 00:30:16,720
All right.

536
00:30:16,790 --> 00:30:18,620
Let's do it.

537
00:30:22,230 --> 00:30:24,560
Come into my office
for a second.

538
00:30:29,570 --> 00:30:31,200
Something is wrong.

539
00:30:31,640 --> 00:30:34,730
A couple of hours ago you miraculously
reunite with the girl of your dreams.

540
00:30:34,810 --> 00:30:37,640
Explain to me why you don't have
an ear-to-ear grin right now.

541
00:30:44,620 --> 00:30:46,590
I lost her number.

542
00:30:46,650 --> 00:30:47,750
What?

543
00:30:49,490 --> 00:30:51,720
The woman on the bus.

544
00:30:51,790 --> 00:30:56,790
I lost her phone number and all
I had was her first name, so,

545
00:30:58,630 --> 00:31:00,730
I'm never going
to see her again.

546
00:31:03,340 --> 00:31:04,670
I'm sorry.

547
00:31:05,340 --> 00:31:07,330
Wow, I'm really sorry.

548
00:31:53,250 --> 00:31:55,050
Your entire world
is turned upside down

549
00:31:55,120 --> 00:31:57,350
and you're thinking
about a woman.

550
00:31:58,960 --> 00:32:00,980
Even if you could
remember the number,

551
00:32:01,060 --> 00:32:02,590
you'll never get through.

552
00:32:02,660 --> 00:32:04,890
Lost cell phone,
changed numbers,

553
00:32:04,970 --> 00:32:07,230
whatever creates
the fewest ripples.

554
00:32:07,900 --> 00:32:09,490
My name is Harry,
by the way.

555
00:32:10,970 --> 00:32:12,960
The fewest ripples?

556
00:32:15,640 --> 00:32:18,610
You freeze people.
You froze my friend.

557
00:32:18,680 --> 00:32:20,580
We need special
authorization to...

558
00:32:20,650 --> 00:32:22,210
Poke around in
people's brains.

559
00:32:22,280 --> 00:32:23,340
You make them think
whatever you want...

560
00:32:23,420 --> 00:32:24,640
Be quiet, David.

561
00:32:24,720 --> 00:32:25,780
Can I get you something?

562
00:32:25,850 --> 00:32:27,790
Just some water, please.

563
00:32:27,850 --> 00:32:29,910
Your friend will be fine.

564
00:32:29,990 --> 00:32:34,320
Recalibrations make tiny
changes in the way people reason.

565
00:32:34,560 --> 00:32:37,030
It doesn't work on
emotion or personality.

566
00:32:37,530 --> 00:32:38,720
That's too intrusive.

567
00:32:38,800 --> 00:32:41,160
You said authorization.
Authorization from who?

568
00:32:41,230 --> 00:32:42,500
The Chairman.

569
00:32:42,600 --> 00:32:43,590
The Chairman?

570
00:32:44,970 --> 00:32:47,030
That's just
a name we use.

571
00:32:47,670 --> 00:32:49,870
You use many other names.

572
00:32:53,480 --> 00:32:54,670
We can't talk here.

573
00:32:56,650 --> 00:33:00,420
Meet me on the 4:00 p. m. boat
and I'll answer what I can.

574
00:33:02,560 --> 00:33:04,920
If you reveal us, even
if it's unintentional...

575
00:33:04,990 --> 00:33:06,750
I know. You'll bump
me on the head.

576
00:33:06,830 --> 00:33:10,790
Richardson has automatic
authority to reset you.

577
00:33:11,560 --> 00:33:13,760
You mean lobotomize me?

578
00:33:13,830 --> 00:33:15,800
Try not to forget it.

579
00:33:15,870 --> 00:33:17,630
Be on the 4:00 p. m. boat.

580
00:33:34,720 --> 00:33:37,620
Ask your questions,
I'll tell you what I can.

581
00:33:38,360 --> 00:33:40,520
What were they
doing to Charlie?

582
00:33:40,590 --> 00:33:43,460
Just changing the way
he weighs investment risks

583
00:33:43,530 --> 00:33:47,660
which will subtly shift the direction
of his company in the way upstairs wants.

584
00:33:47,870 --> 00:33:49,960
So, Richardson could
read my mind?

585
00:33:50,040 --> 00:33:51,900
Richardson was trying
to scare you.

586
00:33:51,970 --> 00:33:54,600
No, he knew the number I
was thinking of, the color.

587
00:33:54,670 --> 00:33:56,470
Because he set it up
as a choice.

588
00:33:56,540 --> 00:33:58,670
"Choose a color,
choose a number. ' '

589
00:33:58,780 --> 00:34:01,750
We can't read your mind
or hear your thoughts.

590
00:34:02,150 --> 00:34:05,450
When you make a decision,
your mind weighs options.

591
00:34:05,520 --> 00:34:06,850
We can perceive them.

592
00:34:06,990 --> 00:34:09,580
We know when you're going to
go off the plan or not because

593
00:34:09,660 --> 00:34:11,210
if we're close enough,

594
00:34:11,290 --> 00:34:13,230
we can sense when
it's going to happen.

595
00:34:13,760 --> 00:34:15,390
We're just here
to keep you on plan.

596
00:34:15,460 --> 00:34:17,330
That's all we're
authorized to do.

597
00:34:17,930 --> 00:34:19,660
Are you allowed to be
telling me this stuff?

598
00:34:19,730 --> 00:34:21,600
Are they following me now?

599
00:34:21,700 --> 00:34:23,730
We have to monitor
the entire world.

600
00:34:24,470 --> 00:34:27,770
We don't have the manpower to
follow everyone all the time.

601
00:34:28,840 --> 00:34:30,780
And there is
something about water.

602
00:34:30,840 --> 00:34:33,710
It blocks our ability
to read your decision tree.

603
00:34:33,850 --> 00:34:35,840
Are you an angel?

604
00:34:36,780 --> 00:34:38,080
We've been called that.

605
00:34:38,590 --> 00:34:40,450
We're more like
case officers

606
00:34:40,590 --> 00:34:43,280
who live a lot longer
than humans.

607
00:34:43,490 --> 00:34:44,960
Why are you helping me?

608
00:34:45,090 --> 00:34:46,650
I have my reasons.

609
00:34:48,190 --> 00:34:49,920
And why won't they
let me be with Elise?

610
00:34:50,500 --> 00:34:54,020
All I know is the amount
of resources they've used

611
00:34:54,100 --> 00:34:57,730
keeping you from her is
pretty damn important to them.

612
00:34:59,840 --> 00:35:02,030
You're going to look
for her, aren't you?

613
00:35:03,040 --> 00:35:04,910
You won't find her.

614
00:35:04,980 --> 00:35:07,110
They'll make sure of it.

615
00:35:07,780 --> 00:35:10,180
<i>Even if they weren't
trying to stop you,</i>

616
00:35:10,250 --> 00:35:13,340
<i>there are nine million
people in this city.</i>

617
00:35:13,420 --> 00:35:15,450
<i>You'll never find her.</i>

618
00:35:16,660 --> 00:35:18,560
<i>Forget about her.</i>

619
00:35:18,630 --> 00:35:20,620
<i>Move on with your life.</i>

620
00:36:40,940 --> 00:36:42,930
Stop!
Stop the bus!

621
00:36:57,190 --> 00:36:59,220
Elise? Elise, hi.

622
00:36:59,290 --> 00:37:01,020
David.

623
00:37:01,090 --> 00:37:05,050
You know, I rode the M6 to
work every day for three years

624
00:37:05,130 --> 00:37:07,730
hoping that I'd
bump into you.

625
00:37:08,900 --> 00:37:11,170
Listen, is there a place
we could go and talk?

626
00:37:13,070 --> 00:37:15,670
Don't you have to get
to work or something?

627
00:37:15,740 --> 00:37:16,870
I just got sick.

628
00:37:16,940 --> 00:37:18,740
"I haven't seen you in three
years, you make me sick. ' '

629
00:37:18,810 --> 00:37:20,680
That's not an ideal come-on,
just so you know.

630
00:37:20,810 --> 00:37:21,940
What are you looking for?

631
00:37:22,020 --> 00:37:23,880
I felt like someone
was watching us.

632
00:37:24,020 --> 00:37:26,580
Hey, Charlie, I'm going
to have to skip breakfast,

633
00:37:26,650 --> 00:37:29,590
and I probably have to postpone the
speech, too. I just bumped into Elise.

634
00:37:29,660 --> 00:37:31,150
You just hung up
on your friend.

635
00:37:31,220 --> 00:37:32,620
We've known each other
since we were kids.

636
00:37:32,690 --> 00:37:33,990
What speech?
It doesn't matter.

637
00:37:34,060 --> 00:37:35,990
You want to take a walk?
No.

638
00:37:36,060 --> 00:37:37,120
We have a lot
to catch up on.

639
00:37:37,200 --> 00:37:38,530
I don't think you should
cancel your speech.

640
00:37:38,600 --> 00:37:40,570
What if I don't like you
at the end of our walk?

641
00:37:40,700 --> 00:37:42,070
I'll take my chances.

642
00:37:42,140 --> 00:37:43,690
Seriously, my number
hasn't changed,

643
00:37:43,770 --> 00:37:45,760
just call me like
you didn't last time.

644
00:37:45,840 --> 00:37:48,570
Look, if you take a walk
with me, I'll explain why.

645
00:37:49,340 --> 00:37:52,900
It's been years. How do you know
I don't have a nice boyfriend?

646
00:37:52,980 --> 00:37:54,880
Do you have
a nice boyfriend?

647
00:37:54,950 --> 00:37:56,280
Would it matter if I did?

648
00:37:56,350 --> 00:37:57,340
Yeah.

649
00:37:57,420 --> 00:37:59,390
So you don't have any
real conviction then?

650
00:37:59,450 --> 00:38:00,750
Okay, it wouldn't
matter at all.

651
00:38:00,820 --> 00:38:02,880
So, you don't mind
being a home-wrecker?

652
00:38:02,960 --> 00:38:04,050
Congressman!

653
00:38:04,120 --> 00:38:06,520
How are you doing, man? I'm
from Red Hook. You're the best.

654
00:38:06,590 --> 00:38:08,560
Thank you.
Good to see you.

655
00:38:09,160 --> 00:38:12,030
If you were married,
then it would bother me.

656
00:38:12,270 --> 00:38:15,200
But now you're just saying
what you think I want to hear.

657
00:38:15,770 --> 00:38:17,200
I'm single.

658
00:38:17,270 --> 00:38:20,210
Now let's hear your bullshit
excuse for not calling me.

659
00:38:20,270 --> 00:38:21,300
I didn't have your number.

660
00:38:21,370 --> 00:38:22,970
I gave it you on the bus.
Handed it to you.

661
00:38:23,040 --> 00:38:24,980
I was mugged.
It was taken from me.

662
00:38:25,040 --> 00:38:26,240
No, come on.

663
00:38:26,310 --> 00:38:28,610
My wallet was taken.
Come on.

664
00:38:28,680 --> 00:38:30,580
Why else would I fawn
all over you on a bus

665
00:38:30,650 --> 00:38:32,020
three years ago and
then never call you?

666
00:38:32,090 --> 00:38:35,020
Because, I don't know! You have a
girlfriend and you felt terrible.

667
00:38:35,090 --> 00:38:38,820
Listen, I swear to you that
I did not have your number.

668
00:38:38,890 --> 00:38:39,950
All right.

669
00:38:40,030 --> 00:38:42,590
No, on my
parents' graves.

670
00:38:42,660 --> 00:38:43,650
That's heavy.

671
00:38:43,730 --> 00:38:45,130
That's too heavy,
I know, but it's true.

672
00:38:45,200 --> 00:38:46,790
I didn't have your number.

673
00:38:46,870 --> 00:38:48,730
And I didn't even have
a last name to go by.

674
00:38:48,800 --> 00:38:50,830
If you google just "Elise,"

675
00:38:50,900 --> 00:38:54,130
you get 757,000 hits.
You did not.

676
00:38:54,210 --> 00:38:55,670
And none of them are you.

677
00:39:06,990 --> 00:39:08,450
We have a problem.

678
00:39:08,520 --> 00:39:09,780
What?

679
00:39:10,060 --> 00:39:11,350
David Norris is off plan.

680
00:39:12,090 --> 00:39:15,190
Son of a bitch.
How did he find her?

681
00:39:15,260 --> 00:39:18,030
Chance. Just spotted
her on the street.

682
00:39:18,100 --> 00:39:20,290
We never should have let him
meet her in the first place.

683
00:39:20,370 --> 00:39:22,600
We followed protocol
to the letter.

684
00:39:22,670 --> 00:39:25,300
Guy rides the same bus
every day for three years.

685
00:39:25,370 --> 00:39:26,860
Who does that?

686
00:39:29,880 --> 00:39:32,440
Three years later, I'm
still cleaning up your mess.

687
00:39:39,850 --> 00:39:42,720
Let's cut the power
at her dance studio.

688
00:39:43,620 --> 00:39:44,720
I'm a dancer.

689
00:39:44,790 --> 00:39:45,820
Oh.

690
00:39:45,890 --> 00:39:47,520
Not like that,
you pervert.

691
00:39:47,590 --> 00:39:49,250
I'm in a contemporary
ballet company.

692
00:39:49,330 --> 00:39:50,990
I think you have
the wrong idea about me.

693
00:39:51,060 --> 00:39:53,620
No, I think that was the first thing
that crossed your mind, actually.

694
00:39:53,770 --> 00:39:56,200
I think you should invite me
to one of your performances.

695
00:39:56,270 --> 00:39:58,070
The company is
called Cedar Lake,

696
00:39:58,140 --> 00:40:01,300
and there is actually a show
tomorrow night, if you want.

697
00:40:01,370 --> 00:40:03,400
Is that an invitation?
No, it's information.

698
00:40:03,540 --> 00:40:05,240
We're covering the girl's
artistic director

699
00:40:05,340 --> 00:40:07,280
and we're still working
on Charlie Traynor.

700
00:40:07,350 --> 00:40:09,180
This whole thing will
be over in an hour.

701
00:40:09,280 --> 00:40:11,380
Hi, how are you?
Paul, right?

702
00:40:11,480 --> 00:40:12,640
How did
you remember that?

703
00:40:12,720 --> 00:40:14,120
I'm a politician,
I'm good with names.

704
00:40:14,190 --> 00:40:16,350
Her decision tree is
diverging from our models.

705
00:40:16,420 --> 00:40:17,410
It's just
the two of you?

706
00:40:17,490 --> 00:40:18,680
Yes.

707
00:40:18,890 --> 00:40:20,260
Thirty-six hours
without contact,

708
00:40:20,330 --> 00:40:21,590
she'll never speak
to him again.

709
00:40:21,660 --> 00:40:22,720
Look at her file.

710
00:40:22,800 --> 00:40:25,060
It's the last thing in the
world she'll put up with.

711
00:40:25,130 --> 00:40:28,260
No, something's wrong. I'm
already seeing inflection points.

712
00:40:31,600 --> 00:40:32,700
You okay?

713
00:40:32,770 --> 00:40:34,030
Yeah.

714
00:40:36,980 --> 00:40:40,140
We're in motion on changing
the location of her rehearsal.

715
00:40:41,650 --> 00:40:43,270
You have to be born
with the right body

716
00:40:43,350 --> 00:40:46,320
like very flexible hips
and shoulders and long neck.

717
00:40:46,390 --> 00:40:47,410
And you weren't?

718
00:40:47,490 --> 00:40:49,150
No, I was.
I was lucky.

719
00:40:49,290 --> 00:40:52,750
But after 15 years of that
precision and the training...

720
00:40:52,830 --> 00:40:54,020
How is everything
over here?

721
00:40:54,090 --> 00:40:55,420
It's great. Thank you.

722
00:40:55,500 --> 00:40:57,220
It's good,
thank you.

723
00:40:59,370 --> 00:41:02,330
So, I wanted to try
something else just on my own,

724
00:41:02,400 --> 00:41:05,100
open-ended and
human, I guess.

725
00:41:05,170 --> 00:41:06,900
What do you mean?

726
00:41:07,970 --> 00:41:09,100
Look.

727
00:41:11,210 --> 00:41:12,700
What's this?

728
00:41:12,780 --> 00:41:14,210
If they kiss.

729
00:41:14,410 --> 00:41:17,010
A kiss?
That's all it takes?

730
00:41:17,080 --> 00:41:18,210
A real kiss.

731
00:41:18,280 --> 00:41:19,910
If that happens, every
possible adjustment

732
00:41:19,990 --> 00:41:21,080
strong enough
to break them up

733
00:41:21,150 --> 00:41:23,710
will cause ripples
over your limit.

734
00:41:24,920 --> 00:41:26,410
I think that everything
you go through in life

735
00:41:26,490 --> 00:41:28,520
should come out in
the way that you dance.

736
00:41:28,660 --> 00:41:30,490
I haven't found that to be
the case with my dancing.

737
00:41:32,470 --> 00:41:33,660
It's a shame.

738
00:41:34,070 --> 00:41:36,690
David,
hate to interrupt.

739
00:41:36,770 --> 00:41:38,700
Hi. Hi, nice to see you again, Elise.

740
00:41:38,810 --> 00:41:39,900
How did you find me?

741
00:41:39,970 --> 00:41:41,130
Does it matter?

742
00:41:42,040 --> 00:41:43,030
Nice work.

743
00:41:43,110 --> 00:41:45,410
There's a crowd of people waiting
for your announcement speech

744
00:41:45,480 --> 00:41:46,780
at the Brooklyn Bridge
right now.

745
00:41:46,850 --> 00:41:48,910
You're announcing today?
You can't skip this for me.

746
00:41:48,980 --> 00:41:51,540
I know. I told you I'm not
going to let you out of my sight.

747
00:41:51,620 --> 00:41:53,520
David, what the hell?
Do you want to come?

748
00:41:53,590 --> 00:41:54,750
Do you want to watch?

749
00:41:54,820 --> 00:41:57,310
No. I can't, I have
to go and rehearse.

750
00:41:57,420 --> 00:41:59,250
Actually, I just heard that
they're moving my rehearsal

751
00:41:59,330 --> 00:42:00,990
to Pier 17, right
next to the bridge.

752
00:42:01,060 --> 00:42:04,030
Why don't we just postpone it?
We'll do the speech another time.

753
00:42:04,100 --> 00:42:05,500
No, David, come on,
you can't do that.

754
00:42:05,570 --> 00:42:06,590
Thank you.

755
00:42:06,670 --> 00:42:07,930
You have to go.
Check.

756
00:42:08,870 --> 00:42:10,460
David, she's
rehearsing so close.

757
00:42:10,540 --> 00:42:12,230
It'll take you three minutes
to get there after the speech.

758
00:42:12,310 --> 00:42:13,500
All right.

759
00:42:13,570 --> 00:42:15,270
Just come over
when you're done.

760
00:42:16,840 --> 00:42:18,110
Keep the change.
Thank you.

761
00:42:18,180 --> 00:42:21,310
Have Charlie's aide
call him now.

762
00:42:23,050 --> 00:42:24,280
Are you sure
you don't want a ride?

763
00:42:24,350 --> 00:42:26,080
No, I have to go
home and change.

764
00:42:26,150 --> 00:42:28,810
You're so late.
Your friend is about to cry.

765
00:42:28,890 --> 00:42:30,250
I'm wrangling him
right now.

766
00:42:31,190 --> 00:42:32,250
Yeah, I know.

767
00:42:33,230 --> 00:42:34,850
Shifting her rehearsal
to Pier 17

768
00:42:34,930 --> 00:42:36,120
was a genius move.

769
00:42:36,400 --> 00:42:38,330
We're not out of
the woods yet.

770
00:42:38,870 --> 00:42:40,030
Come over when
you're done.

771
00:42:43,000 --> 00:42:44,590
David.
Okay.

772
00:42:44,670 --> 00:42:47,100
Sorry, the TV crew is there.
We got to go, man.

773
00:42:53,150 --> 00:42:54,540
We're okay.

774
00:42:56,750 --> 00:42:57,910
Look, whatever happens,

775
00:42:57,980 --> 00:43:00,150
I'm not going to let anything
come between us again.

776
00:43:01,220 --> 00:43:02,880
Okay.

777
00:43:04,060 --> 00:43:05,610
I'll see you soon.

778
00:43:13,800 --> 00:43:15,360
Hey, how you doing?

779
00:43:16,170 --> 00:43:18,300
Thank you.
Appreciate it.

780
00:43:18,370 --> 00:43:19,960
How are you?

781
00:43:21,940 --> 00:43:23,670
Thanks a lot.
Good to see you, too.

782
00:43:26,750 --> 00:43:28,210
<i>Thank you.</i>

783
00:43:31,750 --> 00:43:34,780
<i>I have to say, it's good
to be back in Brooklyn.</i>

784
00:43:37,020 --> 00:43:38,750
<i>There's been a lot
of speculation</i>

785
00:43:38,820 --> 00:43:40,310
<i>as to whether or not...
McCRADY: It's done.</i>

786
00:43:40,390 --> 00:43:42,830
<i>...I would run for a Senate seat
here in the state of New York.</i>

787
00:43:42,960 --> 00:43:45,520
<i>I came here today to put
an end to that speculation</i>

788
00:43:45,600 --> 00:43:47,500
<i>and to tell you,
unequivocally,</i>

789
00:43:47,570 --> 00:43:51,660
<i>I will seek the Senate seat
for the great state of New York.</i>

790
00:43:55,040 --> 00:43:57,700
David! David!

791
00:44:08,520 --> 00:44:09,950
Shit.

792
00:44:12,360 --> 00:44:13,830
Don't forget, I added
the bankers' group

793
00:44:13,890 --> 00:44:15,420
<i>after The Daily Show
tomorrow.</i>

794
00:44:17,300 --> 00:44:18,700
Are you listening to me?

795
00:44:21,030 --> 00:44:22,060
David?

796
00:44:26,740 --> 00:44:29,000
I cannot get a break
on this case.

797
00:44:33,710 --> 00:44:36,940
Okay. I got
to get to Pier 17.

798
00:44:37,020 --> 00:44:39,110
Do the interview and you
can do whatever you want.

799
00:44:39,190 --> 00:44:41,090
Just handle the interviews for
me. I'll talk to those guys...

800
00:44:41,150 --> 00:44:42,450
This has got to go
on the nightly news.

801
00:44:42,520 --> 00:44:43,990
I got to see Elise
right now.

802
00:44:44,060 --> 00:44:45,110
Every time we get close,

803
00:44:45,190 --> 00:44:47,160
you do something
to mess this up.

804
00:44:48,290 --> 00:44:49,780
This is different.

805
00:45:13,150 --> 00:45:14,180
<i>City, please?</i>

806
00:45:14,250 --> 00:45:15,350
Manhattan.

807
00:45:15,420 --> 00:45:16,750
<i>What listing?</i>

808
00:45:16,820 --> 00:45:18,690
Cedar Lake.
It's a dance company.

809
00:45:20,490 --> 00:45:21,760
<i>Connecting.</i>

810
00:45:34,570 --> 00:45:37,100
He's going to ask the parking
attendant to use the hard line.

811
00:45:37,180 --> 00:45:38,700
I already killed it.

812
00:45:38,840 --> 00:45:40,180
Excuse me,
can I use your phone?

813
00:45:40,250 --> 00:45:41,800
I have an emergency.

814
00:45:48,390 --> 00:45:49,720
McCRADY: Restaurant
across the street.

815
00:45:49,790 --> 00:45:50,950
Mmm-hmm.

816
00:45:51,360 --> 00:45:53,420
I took out everything
for three blocks.

817
00:45:53,490 --> 00:45:55,220
This guy is
a pain in the ass.

818
00:45:55,290 --> 00:45:58,160
Come on,
let's go talk to him.

819
00:46:01,070 --> 00:46:03,040
There is a whole world
of women out there.

820
00:46:03,100 --> 00:46:04,970
I thought we established
this one was off limits.

821
00:46:05,640 --> 00:46:08,200
It's been a while,
I must have forgotten.

822
00:46:08,270 --> 00:46:10,240
Doesn't change the fact.

823
00:46:11,640 --> 00:46:12,910
You put us
together three times.

824
00:46:13,350 --> 00:46:15,370
That wasn't us,
that was just chance.

825
00:46:16,780 --> 00:46:18,040
Why do you want
to keep us apart?

826
00:46:18,120 --> 00:46:19,380
Because the plan says so.

827
00:46:19,450 --> 00:46:20,980
Then you misread the plan.

828
00:46:21,050 --> 00:46:23,350
There is no misreading the plan
when it comes to you and Elise.

829
00:46:23,420 --> 00:46:24,550
Then the plan is wrong.

830
00:46:24,620 --> 00:46:25,990
Do you know who wrote it?
I don't care.

831
00:46:26,060 --> 00:46:29,360
You should. You should
really show a little respect.

832
00:46:33,370 --> 00:46:34,700
If I'm not supposed
to be with her,

833
00:46:34,770 --> 00:46:36,030
how come I feel like this?

834
00:46:36,170 --> 00:46:38,260
It doesn't matter
how you feel.

835
00:46:38,340 --> 00:46:40,860
What matters is what
is in black and white.

836
00:46:46,910 --> 00:46:49,880
You don't know why I'm not
supposed to be with her, do you?

837
00:46:51,480 --> 00:46:54,280
That's why you can't
tell me. You don't know.

838
00:46:56,160 --> 00:46:57,750
Who is this guy?

839
00:46:59,020 --> 00:47:00,360
Uh-oh.

840
00:47:00,430 --> 00:47:02,190
Excuse me, everyone.

841
00:47:02,260 --> 00:47:05,530
Sorry to disturb your lunch.
It's an emergency.

842
00:47:05,600 --> 00:47:09,800
Has anyone here ever seen a performance
at the Cedar Lake Ballet Company?

843
00:47:10,770 --> 00:47:12,630
The Cedar Lake
Ballet Company?

844
00:47:12,710 --> 00:47:13,690
Yeah, I have.

845
00:47:13,810 --> 00:47:14,930
Where is it?

846
00:47:15,010 --> 00:47:16,600
Son of a bitch.

847
00:47:25,820 --> 00:47:27,880
Hey, taxi. Hey!

848
00:47:32,790 --> 00:47:34,380
Taxi! Hey!

849
00:47:41,270 --> 00:47:42,670
Taxi!

850
00:47:44,840 --> 00:47:46,670
Maybe you should
try the subway.

851
00:47:46,740 --> 00:47:49,640
Don't all these taxies you're
diverting have plans, too?

852
00:47:49,710 --> 00:47:53,370
How long you going to keep that
up? Twenty minutes? An hour?

853
00:47:53,450 --> 00:47:56,180
Wow, the ripples must
just be endless.

854
00:47:56,250 --> 00:47:59,240
I don't care what you put
in my way, I'm not giving up.

855
00:48:03,720 --> 00:48:05,710
Hey! Hey! Hey!

856
00:48:09,430 --> 00:48:10,590
Taxi!

857
00:48:10,660 --> 00:48:12,060
Hey!

858
00:48:18,540 --> 00:48:19,900
You all right?

859
00:48:23,110 --> 00:48:24,130
You okay?

860
00:48:24,210 --> 00:48:25,340
What happened?

861
00:48:25,410 --> 00:48:27,440
You got hit.
Are you okay?

862
00:48:27,510 --> 00:48:28,910
I think so.
Don't move.

863
00:48:32,420 --> 00:48:33,940
You all right?

864
00:48:35,490 --> 00:48:38,120
Hello, I just
witnessed an accident

865
00:48:38,190 --> 00:48:40,520
on the corner of
Water and Dover Street.

866
00:48:40,590 --> 00:48:42,690
Unfortunately, you're the
only witness, Congressman.

867
00:48:42,760 --> 00:48:43,960
But we did this
three times already.

868
00:48:44,030 --> 00:48:45,500
I just need
a few more details.

869
00:48:46,570 --> 00:48:47,790
Oh, I see.

870
00:48:47,870 --> 00:48:49,860
You're not even a cop, are
you? You're one of them.

871
00:48:49,940 --> 00:48:51,770
Calm down.
Okay.

872
00:48:51,840 --> 00:48:53,270
Someone could
have been killed.

873
00:48:53,340 --> 00:48:54,830
Sir, if you would
just calm down.

874
00:48:54,910 --> 00:48:56,240
Hey, Sergeant.

875
00:48:56,410 --> 00:48:58,810
Hi. Sorry, excuse me, I know
you need a statement from me,

876
00:48:58,910 --> 00:49:00,810
but do I need to stay here
or can I do it over the phone?

877
00:49:00,880 --> 00:49:02,680
No, sir, we know how
to get in touch with you.

878
00:49:02,750 --> 00:49:05,550
Okay. Now, is this man
under your command?

879
00:49:07,350 --> 00:49:08,380
Never mind. Thank you.

880
00:49:19,500 --> 00:49:21,020
Hey, hey, hey!

881
00:49:22,600 --> 00:49:23,970
You want to make
a quick 100 bucks?

882
00:49:24,040 --> 00:49:26,560
You're going to have to break
a couple of traffic laws.

883
00:49:26,640 --> 00:49:30,080
All right, 26th and 10th. It's a
ballet company called Cedar Lake.

884
00:49:35,310 --> 00:49:36,580
Go through that.

885
00:49:37,280 --> 00:49:38,340
Look out!

886
00:49:38,420 --> 00:49:39,780
Watch it.

887
00:49:40,820 --> 00:49:43,380
This is going to keep
happening, just go through.

888
00:49:47,060 --> 00:49:48,190
Son of a bitch!

889
00:49:49,900 --> 00:49:51,990
Set a traffic jam on
Broadway. Too many ripples.

890
00:49:52,060 --> 00:49:53,330
He's getting out of range.

891
00:49:53,400 --> 00:49:55,890
I don't know what to do with
this. You're reaching your limit.

892
00:49:57,370 --> 00:49:58,430
Hey!

893
00:49:59,470 --> 00:50:00,670
Excuse me.

894
00:50:05,510 --> 00:50:07,840
Two down. Two down.

895
00:50:09,320 --> 00:50:11,040
Oh, shit!

896
00:50:11,120 --> 00:50:12,480
Next one.

897
00:50:12,550 --> 00:50:14,310
I hate downtown.

898
00:50:23,560 --> 00:50:25,000
Sorry.

899
00:50:27,730 --> 00:50:29,390
I've got
inflection points.

900
00:50:29,470 --> 00:50:32,270
All he needs to do is see her dance
and you're at your ripple limit.

901
00:50:32,340 --> 00:50:34,430
What? All right,
block the door.

902
00:50:34,510 --> 00:50:35,630
He's already
through it.

903
00:50:35,710 --> 00:50:36,940
Not that one,
the next one.

904
00:50:37,010 --> 00:50:38,170
Excuse me.

905
00:50:38,240 --> 00:50:40,010
David Norris,
I'm here to see Elise.

906
00:50:40,080 --> 00:50:41,880
I'm blocking them all.

907
00:50:43,350 --> 00:50:44,710
Hat! Hat!

908
00:50:44,780 --> 00:50:46,910
Is she here?
Yeah, she's in the studio.

909
00:50:51,760 --> 00:50:54,490
McCRADY: It's too late.
He's already seen her.

910
00:50:55,830 --> 00:50:57,760
You hit your ripple limit.

911
00:50:58,660 --> 00:51:00,130
Celebrate now.

912
00:51:00,670 --> 00:51:02,460
They're just going
to kick it upstairs.

913
00:51:02,530 --> 00:51:04,000
It's Donaldson.

914
00:51:09,780 --> 00:51:12,010
He has no idea
what he's up against.

915
00:51:57,160 --> 00:51:59,180
Let's take a walk.

916
00:52:05,060 --> 00:52:07,790
The intense chemistry
between them,

917
00:52:07,870 --> 00:52:09,770
the constant
inflection points,

918
00:52:09,840 --> 00:52:12,360
and the kicker, you
pulling them apart twice

919
00:52:12,440 --> 00:52:15,070
only to have chance
put them back together.

920
00:52:15,940 --> 00:52:17,770
Yeah, all that
seemed a little much.

921
00:52:17,840 --> 00:52:21,570
So, I asked Burdensky to do
some research this morning.

922
00:52:21,650 --> 00:52:24,670
Have you been
in here before?

923
00:52:24,820 --> 00:52:26,410
No, of course not.

924
00:52:26,490 --> 00:52:28,650
It seems like David
Norris and Elise Sellas

925
00:52:28,720 --> 00:52:29,740
were meant to be together

926
00:52:29,820 --> 00:52:32,660
because they were
meant to be together.

927
00:52:33,530 --> 00:52:34,860
Wait, what?

928
00:52:34,990 --> 00:52:36,290
In the '70s
when he was born,

929
00:52:36,360 --> 00:52:37,730
they were meant
to be together,

930
00:52:37,800 --> 00:52:40,160
same thing
in the '80s, '90s.

931
00:52:40,230 --> 00:52:42,790
Wasn't till 2005
that the plan changed

932
00:52:42,870 --> 00:52:46,000
and she was supposed
to stay with Adrian.

933
00:52:51,980 --> 00:52:55,110
<i>The problem here is there are
remnants from all those old plans</i>

934
00:52:55,180 --> 00:52:57,650
<i>that keep pushing
them together.</i>

935
00:52:57,720 --> 00:53:00,910
<i>They still feel like they belong with
each other even though they don't,</i>

936
00:53:01,020 --> 00:53:03,720
<i>and we're about to tear
them apart again.</i>

937
00:53:17,740 --> 00:53:20,030
You spend
your whole career

938
00:53:20,110 --> 00:53:23,270
hoping, someday,
you'll get a red-letter case.

939
00:53:24,080 --> 00:53:26,340
Something you can really
make a name for yourself with.

940
00:53:27,610 --> 00:53:29,380
Finally you get one,

941
00:53:29,780 --> 00:53:31,250
and it's booby-trapped.

942
00:53:31,750 --> 00:53:33,010
What?

943
00:53:33,090 --> 00:53:35,820
I'm saying,
it wasn't your fault.

944
00:53:36,990 --> 00:53:38,550
They were meant
to be together.

945
00:53:39,020 --> 00:53:40,080
What?

946
00:53:40,160 --> 00:53:42,680
Were. In an earlier
version of the plan.

947
00:53:42,760 --> 00:53:45,030
Actually, a dozen
earlier versions.

948
00:53:46,830 --> 00:53:49,490
I always wondered
how we had such bad luck

949
00:53:49,570 --> 00:53:54,160
that she was on that particular
bus on that particular day.

950
00:53:54,640 --> 00:53:56,800
How could a plan
just change like that?

951
00:53:56,940 --> 00:53:58,880
I don't know.

952
00:53:58,940 --> 00:54:01,640
It's above my pay grade.

953
00:54:02,010 --> 00:54:03,540
So, we're going
operational.

954
00:54:03,620 --> 00:54:05,110
This is exciting.
It's been a while.

955
00:54:05,250 --> 00:54:08,580
We're not
going operational.

956
00:54:09,760 --> 00:54:13,190
No one ever made it to my
job by taking stupid risks.

957
00:54:13,690 --> 00:54:15,020
So, what are
we going to do?

958
00:54:15,090 --> 00:54:16,920
We're going to kick
this case upstairs

959
00:54:17,000 --> 00:54:20,020
to someone with the latitude
to clean up this mess

960
00:54:20,100 --> 00:54:21,690
without breaking
into a sweat.

961
00:54:21,770 --> 00:54:23,860
We're going to bring
this to Thompson.

962
00:54:23,940 --> 00:54:25,160
Thompson?

963
00:54:25,900 --> 00:54:28,000
When he was in the field,
his nickname was...

964
00:54:28,070 --> 00:54:29,700
"The Hammer," yes.

965
00:54:29,780 --> 00:54:33,040
He'll crush this little
romance with a flick of his hand

966
00:54:33,180 --> 00:54:34,770
<i>and force them
back on plan again.</i>

967
00:54:34,850 --> 00:54:36,810
I want to go to a club.

968
00:54:36,950 --> 00:54:39,010
Oh! Why ruin
a really nice day?

969
00:54:39,080 --> 00:54:40,810
Come on.
No, I don't dance.

970
00:54:40,890 --> 00:54:43,860
Everyone can dance.
You just feel the music.

971
00:54:43,920 --> 00:54:46,620
Well, I've been told that
I have trouble feeling.

972
00:54:46,690 --> 00:54:48,160
Who told you that?

973
00:54:48,230 --> 00:54:50,160
A long list of women.

974
00:54:50,230 --> 00:54:51,420
That's gross.

975
00:54:51,500 --> 00:54:52,720
You know what?

976
00:54:52,970 --> 00:54:55,300
Let's race to
that lamppost.

977
00:54:55,370 --> 00:54:57,800
You win,
I dance for you.

978
00:54:58,270 --> 00:55:01,330
I win,
you dance for me.

979
00:55:01,410 --> 00:55:02,960
On the pole?

980
00:55:03,040 --> 00:55:04,340
If you like, yeah.

981
00:55:04,410 --> 00:55:06,170
This is serious.
What are the rules?

982
00:55:06,240 --> 00:55:07,610
There are no rules.

983
00:55:07,680 --> 00:55:09,910
Oh! You bastard!
Oh, my gosh!

984
00:55:12,780 --> 00:55:14,380
You challenged me
in those shoes?

985
00:55:14,450 --> 00:55:15,980
Okay, fine. I can't.

986
00:55:16,050 --> 00:55:18,650
You're right. These are the wrong
shoes. I'm going to twist an ankle.

987
00:55:18,720 --> 00:55:20,750
You said, "No rules. ' '
I avoided them.

988
00:55:20,890 --> 00:55:21,980
Ow!

989
00:55:24,060 --> 00:55:26,220
You said you
had trouble feeling.

990
00:55:27,870 --> 00:55:29,060
Great.

991
00:55:35,670 --> 00:55:36,770
Hey, David.

992
00:55:36,840 --> 00:55:38,280
You're even
cuter in person.

993
00:55:38,340 --> 00:55:39,870
Hey,
it's David Norris.

994
00:55:39,980 --> 00:55:42,780
How you doing?
How are you?

995
00:55:42,850 --> 00:55:44,510
How are we
feeling about this tie?

996
00:55:44,620 --> 00:55:45,740
Hey, David Norris!

997
00:55:45,820 --> 00:55:47,150
Hey, how you doing?

998
00:55:47,220 --> 00:55:48,780
Maybe I should go home
and get my tux.

999
00:55:48,850 --> 00:55:51,320
Definitely don't
do that. It's good.

1000
00:55:51,390 --> 00:55:54,790
I should never have come back here
again without my backup dancers.

1001
00:55:58,860 --> 00:55:59,960
I voted for you.

1002
00:56:00,030 --> 00:56:01,190
How are you?

1003
00:56:10,740 --> 00:56:13,610
Norris,
you're a rock star!

1004
00:56:19,850 --> 00:56:21,610
Did you grow up over here?

1005
00:56:21,690 --> 00:56:25,320
Yeah, three blocks over
that way. Pioneer Street.

1006
00:56:25,390 --> 00:56:26,580
My whole childhood.

1007
00:56:26,660 --> 00:56:28,720
What were you
like as a kid?

1008
00:56:29,390 --> 00:56:32,160
I've spent about half my
time in the principal's office

1009
00:56:32,230 --> 00:56:33,390
up until the sixth grade.

1010
00:56:33,470 --> 00:56:34,450
Wow.

1011
00:56:34,530 --> 00:56:37,500
Yeah, that was when my
mother and my brother died

1012
00:56:37,640 --> 00:56:39,040
within a month
of each other.

1013
00:56:39,100 --> 00:56:40,090
I'm sorry.

1014
00:56:40,170 --> 00:56:42,000
No, it's fine.

1015
00:56:42,340 --> 00:56:43,330
You know how I always
tell that story

1016
00:56:43,410 --> 00:56:46,040
about how I got inspired
to go into politics

1017
00:56:46,110 --> 00:56:48,240
because my dad took me
to the Senate Gallery

1018
00:56:48,310 --> 00:56:50,870
and we sat together
and that's true.

1019
00:56:51,350 --> 00:56:53,280
But what I leave out is

1020
00:56:53,350 --> 00:56:55,380
he did that right
after they died.

1021
00:56:55,690 --> 00:56:58,620
And I was bottoming out.

1022
00:56:59,990 --> 00:57:02,650
So, he wanted to get me out
of here for a little while

1023
00:57:02,730 --> 00:57:05,530
and he planned
this road trip to DC,

1024
00:57:05,600 --> 00:57:07,720
and I think it's because
JFK was his idol.

1025
00:57:09,100 --> 00:57:11,000
And we went and
sat in the gallery.

1026
00:57:11,070 --> 00:57:15,370
I remember watching
him watch them,

1027
00:57:15,440 --> 00:57:18,410
and I knew that that's
what I wanted to do.

1028
00:57:20,350 --> 00:57:22,870
I wish they were here
to see all this.

1029
00:57:26,550 --> 00:57:28,020
I was...

1030
00:57:31,990 --> 00:57:34,620
Didn't expect
to tell you any of that.

1031
00:58:49,670 --> 00:58:51,300
David?

1032
00:58:52,900 --> 00:58:54,430
David?

1033
00:59:01,150 --> 00:59:03,380
<i>Hello?
It's Adrian.</i>

1034
00:59:04,750 --> 00:59:06,740
Hey.

1035
00:59:07,950 --> 00:59:10,320
What are you doing?
Why are you calling?

1036
00:59:11,190 --> 00:59:12,680
Hi.

1037
00:59:13,460 --> 00:59:15,020
No, I was...

1038
00:59:15,930 --> 00:59:17,490
Because I was sleeping.

1039
00:59:19,460 --> 00:59:20,950
No.

1040
00:59:21,030 --> 00:59:22,590
No, it's not.

1041
00:59:22,670 --> 00:59:24,660
Why are you calling?

1042
00:59:27,540 --> 00:59:29,630
Okay, well,
I got to go.

1043
00:59:29,710 --> 00:59:32,700
No, I got to go. Okay.

1044
00:59:35,650 --> 00:59:36,810
I'm so sorry.

1045
00:59:36,880 --> 00:59:38,010
It's all right.
Everything okay?

1046
00:59:38,080 --> 00:59:39,240
Yeah.

1047
00:59:40,190 --> 00:59:42,020
Oh, my God. That was so weird.

1048
00:59:42,090 --> 00:59:44,720
I woke up and
you weren't here.

1049
00:59:44,790 --> 00:59:47,820
And then the phone rang
and it was my ex

1050
00:59:47,890 --> 00:59:50,360
which is just so strange.

1051
00:59:50,630 --> 00:59:52,530
Sorry, I guess we hadn't
gotten to any of that stuff yet.

1052
00:59:52,600 --> 00:59:53,660
Right.

1053
00:59:53,730 --> 00:59:56,360
You know, we broke up a while ago,

1054
00:59:56,470 --> 00:59:59,900
and it's over and then he
calls four times in one morning.

1055
01:00:02,740 --> 01:00:04,610
He called you four
times this morning?

1056
01:00:04,680 --> 01:00:05,800
Mmm-hmm.

1057
01:00:07,910 --> 01:00:09,900
How long have you guys
been broken up?

1058
01:00:10,180 --> 01:00:11,210
Um...

1059
01:00:11,480 --> 01:00:13,010
Three months.

1060
01:00:13,990 --> 01:00:16,150
It's like he knew
I was with someone else.

1061
01:00:20,030 --> 01:00:21,150
Was it serious?

1062
01:00:21,230 --> 01:00:23,060
We were engaged.

1063
01:00:24,400 --> 01:00:26,520
So, not really
that serious?

1064
01:00:26,600 --> 01:00:27,930
Right.

1065
01:00:28,570 --> 01:00:30,560
Well, what happened?

1066
01:00:32,340 --> 01:00:33,670
Do you want to know this?

1067
01:00:33,740 --> 01:00:34,860
Yeah.

1068
01:00:38,780 --> 01:00:40,940
He was a great guy.

1069
01:00:41,010 --> 01:00:43,740
Brilliant choreographer
and dancer,

1070
01:00:46,520 --> 01:00:48,110
and we had the same
group of friends.

1071
01:00:48,190 --> 01:00:50,280
We had known each
other a long time.

1072
01:00:51,890 --> 01:00:55,690
Well, he sounds great.
Why didn't you marry him?

1073
01:01:01,570 --> 01:01:03,120
Because of you.

1074
01:01:05,700 --> 01:01:07,730
I'm not some
hopeless romantic.

1075
01:01:07,810 --> 01:01:10,070
I've never allowed myself
to be that way.

1076
01:01:11,640 --> 01:01:13,510
But once I felt,

1077
01:01:14,280 --> 01:01:17,310
even for a moment,
what I felt with you.

1078
01:01:20,150 --> 01:01:23,610
You ruined me. I didn't
want to settle for less.

1079
01:01:24,720 --> 01:01:26,590
I know the feeling.

1080
01:01:26,660 --> 01:01:29,180
It scares the
shit out of me.

1081
01:01:29,290 --> 01:01:31,290
I'm not going
to hurt you.

1082
01:01:33,000 --> 01:01:34,260
You don't need to say that.

1083
01:01:34,330 --> 01:01:36,390
I'm not going
to hurt you.

1084
01:01:40,240 --> 01:01:42,800
This is the first time

1085
01:01:42,870 --> 01:01:45,930
in 25 years that I don't
feel like I'm by myself.

1086
01:01:47,250 --> 01:01:49,210
That's a lot of
responsibility for me.

1087
01:01:49,280 --> 01:01:52,220
I don't know if I'm quite
comfortable with that.

1088
01:01:53,720 --> 01:01:55,310
Well, too late.

1089
01:02:00,960 --> 01:02:04,190
You know, I'm supposed to go
to an interview right now.

1090
01:02:06,770 --> 01:02:10,700
What if I told you I wasn't ready
to let you out of my sight quite yet?

1091
01:02:11,170 --> 01:02:12,970
Sounds good.
You want to go?

1092
01:02:13,040 --> 01:02:14,970
Yeah.
All right.

1093
01:02:15,310 --> 01:02:17,870
<i>Are you handsome</i>

1094
01:02:17,940 --> 01:02:21,640
<i>or is it that Washington
is so populated by</i>

1095
01:02:21,980 --> 01:02:25,280
<i>I don't want to say,
hideous abnormalities...</i>

1096
01:02:25,680 --> 01:02:26,950
This is getting
a little weird.

1097
01:02:28,790 --> 01:02:30,520
David Norris,
everybody.

1098
01:02:37,400 --> 01:02:38,790
Excuse me, ma'am.
Yes?

1099
01:02:38,860 --> 01:02:40,920
Congressman Norris asked me
to give you a message.

1100
01:02:41,100 --> 01:02:42,160
He was just called
into a meeting.

1101
01:02:42,230 --> 01:02:43,560
He says it's urgent.
Okay.

1102
01:02:43,700 --> 01:02:45,230
He said he'll call you
as soon as he's out

1103
01:02:45,300 --> 01:02:46,630
and he'll see you
at the show tonight.

1104
01:02:46,700 --> 01:02:48,800
Okay, thank you.
Thanks.

1105
01:02:57,420 --> 01:02:58,750
This way, Congressman.

1106
01:03:13,930 --> 01:03:16,130
Hello?

1107
01:03:20,770 --> 01:03:22,930
Frustrating, isn't it?

1108
01:03:24,340 --> 01:03:26,040
My name is Thompson.

1109
01:03:28,310 --> 01:03:30,210
Whatever happened
to free will?

1110
01:03:30,280 --> 01:03:32,980
We actually tried
free will before.

1111
01:03:33,050 --> 01:03:35,610
After taking you from
hunting and gathering

1112
01:03:35,690 --> 01:03:37,180
to the height
of the Roman Empire,

1113
01:03:37,260 --> 01:03:41,950
we stepped back to see how
you would do on your own.

1114
01:03:43,160 --> 01:03:46,260
You gave us the Dark Ages
for five centuries

1115
01:03:46,970 --> 01:03:50,060
until finally we decided
we should come back in.

1116
01:03:50,140 --> 01:03:53,900
The Chairman thought that maybe
we just needed to do a better job

1117
01:03:53,970 --> 01:03:55,940
with teaching you
how to ride a bike

1118
01:03:56,110 --> 01:03:58,040
before taking the
training wheels off again.

1119
01:03:58,810 --> 01:04:01,110
So, we gave you
the Renaissance,

1120
01:04:01,180 --> 01:04:03,650
the Enlightenment,
scientific revolution.

1121
01:04:03,780 --> 01:04:05,370
For 600 years
we taught you

1122
01:04:05,450 --> 01:04:07,710
to control your
impulses with reason.

1123
01:04:07,950 --> 01:04:12,390
Then in 1910,
we stepped back again.

1124
01:04:12,460 --> 01:04:17,160
Within 50 years, you had
brought us World War I,

1125
01:04:17,800 --> 01:04:21,200
the Depression,
Fascism, the Holocaust,

1126
01:04:21,270 --> 01:04:23,530
and capped it off by
bringing the entire planet

1127
01:04:23,600 --> 01:04:24,970
to the brink of destruction

1128
01:04:25,100 --> 01:04:26,830
in the Cuban missile crisis.

1129
01:04:26,910 --> 01:04:29,700
At that point a decision was
taken to step back in again

1130
01:04:29,770 --> 01:04:33,870
before you did something
that even we couldn't fix.

1131
01:04:35,350 --> 01:04:38,040
You don't have
free will, David.

1132
01:04:38,850 --> 01:04:41,550
You have the appearance
of free will.

1133
01:04:42,890 --> 01:04:44,050
You expect me
to believe that?

1134
01:04:44,120 --> 01:04:45,350
I make decisions
every day.

1135
01:04:45,420 --> 01:04:47,790
You have free will over
which toothpaste you use

1136
01:04:47,860 --> 01:04:49,790
or which beverage
to order at lunch.

1137
01:04:49,860 --> 01:04:53,060
But humanity just
isn't mature enough

1138
01:04:53,230 --> 01:04:54,990
to control the
important things.

1139
01:04:55,070 --> 01:04:57,330
So, you handle
the important things?

1140
01:04:57,400 --> 01:04:59,770
The last time I checked, the
world is a pretty screwed-up place.

1141
01:04:59,900 --> 01:05:02,200
It's still here.

1142
01:05:02,270 --> 01:05:04,870
If we had left things in
your hands, it wouldn't be.

1143
01:05:04,980 --> 01:05:06,770
Tell me why
I can't be with Elise?

1144
01:05:07,910 --> 01:05:09,380
Because the last
guy didn't know.

1145
01:05:09,450 --> 01:05:13,140
Meeting Elise at the Waldorf
three years ago wasn't chance.

1146
01:05:13,220 --> 01:05:14,650
That was us.

1147
01:05:14,750 --> 01:05:17,620
We knew she would inspire
you to give that speech.

1148
01:05:17,760 --> 01:05:20,950
That speech that brought you
back from the edge of oblivion

1149
01:05:21,030 --> 01:05:24,860
and overnight made you the
frontrunner in this coming election.

1150
01:05:24,930 --> 01:05:26,920
What are you saying? You
want me to win the election?

1151
01:05:27,000 --> 01:05:30,260
This one and
four more after it.

1152
01:05:31,040 --> 01:05:34,490
And I'm not just talking
about elections for Senate.

1153
01:05:35,610 --> 01:05:38,580
You can matter, David.
Really matter.

1154
01:05:39,240 --> 01:05:42,040
What your father wanted when he
took you to the Senate Gallery

1155
01:05:42,110 --> 01:05:44,050
when you were 10.

1156
01:05:44,150 --> 01:05:46,240
What your brother wanted
when he made you promise

1157
01:05:46,320 --> 01:05:48,080
the day before he overdosed

1158
01:05:48,150 --> 01:05:50,210
that you wouldn't
be like him.

1159
01:05:50,290 --> 01:05:54,950
Why do you think you have that
yearning to be in front of people?

1160
01:05:56,660 --> 01:05:59,560
That terrible emptiness
when you're not.

1161
01:05:59,630 --> 01:06:01,060
Don't do that.

1162
01:06:01,130 --> 01:06:03,660
David, you can
change the world,

1163
01:06:03,740 --> 01:06:07,070
but that doesn't happen
if you stay with her.

1164
01:06:07,140 --> 01:06:09,370
Why do you people
care who I love?

1165
01:06:09,440 --> 01:06:12,410
It's not about her,
it's about you.

1166
01:06:12,480 --> 01:06:15,210
What being with her
does to you.

1167
01:06:15,280 --> 01:06:16,710
What it does to me?

1168
01:06:16,780 --> 01:06:17,980
I'm better when
I'm with her!

1169
01:06:18,050 --> 01:06:19,780
Even you said it.
The speech.

1170
01:06:19,920 --> 01:06:23,010
In small doses,
Elise was the cure.

1171
01:06:23,090 --> 01:06:24,750
But in large doses,

1172
01:06:24,820 --> 01:06:26,150
she rubs off on you.

1173
01:06:26,220 --> 01:06:27,210
Stop.

1174
01:06:27,290 --> 01:06:31,250
David, the president
can't be a loose cannon.

1175
01:06:31,330 --> 01:06:32,760
Stop talking.
It's not working.

1176
01:06:32,830 --> 01:06:36,770
Why do you refuse to accept what
should be completely obvious by now?

1177
01:06:36,830 --> 01:06:39,060
You've seen
what we can do.

1178
01:06:39,140 --> 01:06:41,900
You can't doubt we are
who we say we are.

1179
01:06:45,080 --> 01:06:49,810
Look, it's not about who
you are, it's about who I am.

1180
01:06:50,880 --> 01:06:52,750
Can't outrun
your fate, David.

1181
01:06:52,820 --> 01:06:56,220
I just disagree with you
about what my fate is.

1182
01:06:57,120 --> 01:07:01,150
I know what I feel for her
and it's not going to change.

1183
01:07:02,360 --> 01:07:06,090
All I have are
the choices that I make.

1184
01:07:07,000 --> 01:07:08,300
And I choose her

1185
01:07:09,400 --> 01:07:10,920
come what may.

1186
01:07:15,970 --> 01:07:18,070
It's 6:20.

1187
01:07:18,140 --> 01:07:21,630
If you leave now,
you can make Elise's show.

1188
01:08:32,580 --> 01:08:35,070
She's a beautiful dancer.

1189
01:08:36,520 --> 01:08:38,610
I thought I made
myself clear.

1190
01:08:39,660 --> 01:08:42,020
There is one more piece to
this I haven't mentioned.

1191
01:08:43,590 --> 01:08:46,260
I guess I just didn't
have the heart.

1192
01:08:50,270 --> 01:08:52,000
If you stay with her,

1193
01:08:52,070 --> 01:08:55,100
it not only kills your
dreams, it kills hers.

1194
01:08:57,280 --> 01:08:58,610
What?

1195
01:08:59,710 --> 01:09:03,310
Elise is about to become one of the
most famous dancers in the country

1196
01:09:03,380 --> 01:09:06,110
and eventually one of the
world's greatest choreographers.

1197
01:09:07,120 --> 01:09:09,090
If she stays with you,

1198
01:09:09,150 --> 01:09:12,150
she ends up teaching
dance to six-year-olds.

1199
01:09:27,810 --> 01:09:29,930
When you look back
at all this, David,

1200
01:09:30,010 --> 01:09:31,800
just remember

1201
01:09:33,240 --> 01:09:35,580
we tried
to reason with you.

1202
01:10:47,150 --> 01:10:49,420
I always hear
people saying,

1203
01:10:50,350 --> 01:10:54,520
"You can't blame yourself for
what happens to other people,"

1204
01:10:54,660 --> 01:10:57,960
but in this case,
really, you can.

1205
01:11:06,770 --> 01:11:08,670
This is what
you do, David.

1206
01:11:08,740 --> 01:11:11,210
This is what you did
getting into a bar fight

1207
01:11:11,280 --> 01:11:13,140
the night you
first got elected.

1208
01:11:13,240 --> 01:11:16,700
<i>What you did to get that embarrassing
photo in The New York Post.</i>

1209
01:11:17,250 --> 01:11:20,880
We give you opportunities
other people would kill for

1210
01:11:20,950 --> 01:11:23,610
and you squander
them with impulse.

1211
01:11:24,260 --> 01:11:27,220
It doesn't take
a genius to see Elise

1212
01:11:27,290 --> 01:11:30,850
isn't exactly
helping you with this.

1213
01:11:31,800 --> 01:11:34,360
<i>It's a sprain, David,</i>

1214
01:11:34,430 --> 01:11:35,920
<i>but if you stay with her,</i>

1215
01:11:36,000 --> 01:11:38,630
you'll take away the only
thing she ever cared about.

1216
01:11:38,700 --> 01:11:39,930
It's up to you.

1217
01:12:01,160 --> 01:12:02,430
It's just a sprain.

1218
01:12:02,465 --> 01:12:02,950
Yeah.

1219
01:12:03,030 --> 01:12:05,960
The doctor said it's going to
be better in a month or less.

1220
01:12:06,030 --> 01:12:08,660
That's totally unexpected.

1221
01:12:08,770 --> 01:12:10,560
I'm so relieved.

1222
01:12:10,640 --> 01:12:12,500
Great. Great.

1223
01:12:13,200 --> 01:12:15,930
You're such
a great dancer,

1224
01:12:16,970 --> 01:12:21,240
and you'll heal up and
just get better and better.

1225
01:12:31,560 --> 01:12:33,250
I'm going to go make
a couple phone calls.

1226
01:12:33,320 --> 01:12:34,520
Okay.

1227
01:12:37,730 --> 01:12:39,250
Okay.

1228
01:12:40,360 --> 01:12:41,730
I'm so happy for you.

1229
01:13:14,430 --> 01:13:17,530
It's done.
Thompson finished it.

1230
01:13:17,800 --> 01:13:19,430
I know.

1231
01:13:22,110 --> 01:13:23,840
You can't let it
get to you

1232
01:13:24,510 --> 01:13:26,340
like it did
with his family.

1233
01:13:26,910 --> 01:13:28,850
This is the job.

1234
01:13:37,660 --> 01:13:39,520
<i>You ever wonder</i>

1235
01:13:40,560 --> 01:13:42,460
<i>if it's right?</i>

1236
01:13:43,030 --> 01:13:44,720
I mean,

1237
01:13:45,460 --> 01:13:47,490
if it's always right?

1238
01:13:49,470 --> 01:13:51,560
<i>Not like I used to.</i>

1239
01:13:52,770 --> 01:13:54,830
Look, Chairman has the plan.

1240
01:13:54,910 --> 01:13:56,670
We only see part of it.

1241
01:14:21,770 --> 01:14:25,170
<i>I was talking to some friends of ours
over in Monroe County this morning,</i>

1242
01:14:25,240 --> 01:14:27,300
<i>and I said something
to them that I think</i>

1243
01:14:27,370 --> 01:14:30,400
<i>a lot of you may have heard me
say before but it bears repeating.</i>

1244
01:14:30,610 --> 01:14:34,480
<i>David Norris, who has spent the last two
days campaigning in the Tri-Lakes area</i>

1245
01:14:34,610 --> 01:14:38,140
<i>received some welcome news yesterday
when the latest New York Times poll</i>

1246
01:14:38,220 --> 01:14:40,620
<i>gave him a 16-point
lead over his opponent</i>

1247
01:14:40,680 --> 01:14:42,950
<i>less than a month
before election day.</i>

1248
01:14:43,350 --> 01:14:45,450
<i>Since his announcement
11 months ago,</i>

1249
01:14:45,520 --> 01:14:47,750
<i>Norris has had
a significant lead...</i>

1250
01:14:47,830 --> 01:14:50,390
Hi. Thanks.
Thanks a lot.

1251
01:14:50,530 --> 01:14:52,500
Yeah, thank you.
I need it. Thank you.

1252
01:14:52,560 --> 01:14:54,760
Who should I make it out to?
My nephew, Miles.

1253
01:14:54,830 --> 01:14:56,270
There you go.
Thank you so much.

1254
01:14:56,400 --> 01:14:58,390
Yeah, yeah, yeah, sure.

1255
01:14:59,440 --> 01:15:01,300
Thank you.
My pleasure. Pleasure.

1256
01:15:01,870 --> 01:15:02,860
Can we get
a picture with you?

1257
01:15:02,940 --> 01:15:04,430
Sure, come on.

1258
01:15:04,840 --> 01:15:06,640
Thank you.
Thanks.

1259
01:15:10,210 --> 01:15:11,740
Hey.

1260
01:15:12,150 --> 01:15:14,810
I want to show you this, because
you would find out eventually.

1261
01:15:14,890 --> 01:15:16,650
I want to be here
when you do.

1262
01:15:20,560 --> 01:15:24,520
She's getting married to
that guy she was engaged to.

1263
01:15:27,230 --> 01:15:28,930
You okay?

1264
01:15:29,800 --> 01:15:31,320
I think I'm going to...

1265
01:15:31,670 --> 01:15:33,140
Take a day off.

1266
01:15:33,200 --> 01:15:35,800
Hell, take a couple.
We're 16 points up.

1267
01:15:36,740 --> 01:15:38,640
Yeah, even I can't
blow a lead that big.

1268
01:16:47,110 --> 01:16:49,840
Congressman,
good to see you again.

1269
01:16:50,650 --> 01:16:51,980
Your friend
just left you this.

1270
01:16:52,050 --> 01:16:53,570
He said you would
be stopping by.

1271
01:17:14,840 --> 01:17:16,900
Why would you
want to see me?

1272
01:17:19,980 --> 01:17:21,440
Thompson was
lying when he said

1273
01:17:21,510 --> 01:17:24,310
you couldn't be with Elise because
she brings out your reckless side.

1274
01:17:24,880 --> 01:17:27,750
Then why? Why do
they care so much?

1275
01:17:28,290 --> 01:17:31,050
Because she's enough, David.

1276
01:17:31,820 --> 01:17:32,950
If you have her,

1277
01:17:33,020 --> 01:17:35,120
you won't need to fill
that void inside of you

1278
01:17:35,260 --> 01:17:37,750
with applause and votes

1279
01:17:37,830 --> 01:17:40,230
and dreams of one day
making it to the White House.

1280
01:17:41,070 --> 01:17:44,160
That's important, but it's not
the only thing that matters.

1281
01:17:46,040 --> 01:17:47,700
Why are you so
different from them?

1282
01:17:56,950 --> 01:17:58,740
Your dad

1283
01:17:59,980 --> 01:18:02,450
was a very smart man.

1284
01:18:04,120 --> 01:18:07,850
He could have been a
lot more. Wanted to be.

1285
01:18:09,230 --> 01:18:11,390
Your brother, too.

1286
01:18:11,730 --> 01:18:14,130
But the plan
didn't call for it.

1287
01:18:15,130 --> 01:18:16,720
What about my mother?

1288
01:18:16,800 --> 01:18:18,170
That wasn't me.

1289
01:18:18,700 --> 01:18:20,600
That wasn't us at all.

1290
01:18:21,110 --> 01:18:22,770
It was just chance?

1291
01:18:26,180 --> 01:18:27,870
I'm sorry.

1292
01:18:31,780 --> 01:18:34,080
You know,
Thompson is not lying.

1293
01:18:35,050 --> 01:18:38,510
They're already talking about me
being a presidential candidate.

1294
01:18:39,020 --> 01:18:41,720
I'm phoning in my speeches
and I'm 16 points up.

1295
01:18:41,790 --> 01:18:43,660
The public loves you.

1296
01:18:43,990 --> 01:18:46,790
Before I met Elise,
that's all I cared about.

1297
01:18:48,770 --> 01:18:50,860
I don't even notice it now.

1298
01:18:56,510 --> 01:18:58,670
I can't stop
thinking about her.

1299
01:19:06,180 --> 01:19:08,080
Do you know where
she's getting married?

1300
01:19:10,250 --> 01:19:13,190
In front of a judge
tomorrow morning.

1301
01:19:14,090 --> 01:19:15,350
Is she happy?

1302
01:19:18,230 --> 01:19:20,750
Thompson won't let you
anywhere near her.

1303
01:19:20,830 --> 01:19:23,320
Not tonight,
not tomorrow, not ever.

1304
01:19:23,400 --> 01:19:25,100
Harry.

1305
01:19:26,170 --> 01:19:27,230
Is she happy?

1306
01:19:31,180 --> 01:19:32,900
I got to try
and get her back.

1307
01:19:33,910 --> 01:19:37,240
Will you help me? Will you
just help me get to her?

1308
01:19:37,310 --> 01:19:39,480
They'll sense you
coming a mile away.

1309
01:19:40,320 --> 01:19:42,440
What if I could move
as fast as you?

1310
01:19:44,050 --> 01:19:46,180
Teach me about the doors.

1311
01:19:48,190 --> 01:19:49,680
The rain will stop
in the next hour,

1312
01:19:49,830 --> 01:19:52,520
and that's the only thing keeping
them from seeing us right now.

1313
01:19:52,600 --> 01:19:55,290
If we're going to do this,
we'll need all night to do it

1314
01:19:55,370 --> 01:19:58,100
and a place surrounded
by water to do it in.

1315
01:19:59,240 --> 01:20:01,860
Always turn the
doorknob clockwise.

1316
01:20:02,440 --> 01:20:04,200
What happens if I turn it the other way?

1317
01:20:04,270 --> 01:20:07,040
You don't want to do that.
That's only for us.

1318
01:20:08,510 --> 01:20:09,980
Put your hand
on my shoulder

1319
01:20:10,050 --> 01:20:12,380
and don't let go until
we cross the threshold.

1320
01:20:12,850 --> 01:20:14,010
Are these ever locked?

1321
01:20:14,080 --> 01:20:16,680
Not when you're wearing one of our hats.

1322
01:20:21,090 --> 01:20:22,150
Keep moving, David.

1323
01:20:28,170 --> 01:20:29,560
Is this...

1324
01:20:29,900 --> 01:20:31,300
It's the Museum
of Modern Art.

1325
01:20:31,370 --> 01:20:32,530
Yeah.

1326
01:20:43,550 --> 01:20:45,140
Close the door.

1327
01:20:51,390 --> 01:20:55,380
This is the city's main pumping
station for downtown Manhattan.

1328
01:20:56,290 --> 01:20:58,450
We're 10 blocks
from the courthouse.

1329
01:21:03,600 --> 01:21:05,230
Wait here.

1330
01:21:23,420 --> 01:21:27,050
Hi, is the floor open?

1331
01:21:27,120 --> 01:21:28,220
You okay, sweetie?

1332
01:21:28,290 --> 01:21:30,280
Yeah, I just...

1333
01:21:31,260 --> 01:21:34,490
I just feel like
being on the floor.

1334
01:21:41,640 --> 01:21:44,570
Lower Manhattan is layer
upon layer of substrate.

1335
01:21:44,640 --> 01:21:47,580
That's what we call the doors
that have been added over time.

1336
01:21:47,640 --> 01:21:52,050
Makes it a lot more complex to
navigate downtown than it is uptown

1337
01:21:52,120 --> 01:21:53,510
and a lot slower.

1338
01:21:53,620 --> 01:21:58,610
Your destination, 60 Centre
Street, Courtroom 300.

1339
01:22:03,630 --> 01:22:04,990
There are thousands
of different ways

1340
01:22:05,060 --> 01:22:07,330
<i>to get to the courthouse
using the doors.</i>

1341
01:22:07,500 --> 01:22:09,760
I want you to learn how
to stay in the substrate

1342
01:22:09,830 --> 01:22:12,320
so Thompson won't be able
to get a fix on you.

1343
01:22:12,670 --> 01:22:15,500
<i>Okay. Just south of
the diner, it's a red door.</i>

1344
01:22:15,610 --> 01:22:18,040
I come out on Broadway,
right by Leonard Street.

1345
01:22:18,110 --> 01:22:19,940
That's good.
Keep going.

1346
01:22:20,140 --> 01:22:22,410
From Leonard,
half a block down.

1347
01:22:22,480 --> 01:22:24,110
Okay, suppose
Thompson's there?

1348
01:22:24,180 --> 01:22:26,580
Okay, I go east
to Lafayette.

1349
01:22:26,650 --> 01:22:28,410
No, it's Crosby
Street there.

1350
01:22:28,520 --> 01:22:30,490
Eight doors? I'm zigzagging
all over the place.

1351
01:22:30,550 --> 01:22:31,680
That will take me
too long.

1352
01:22:31,760 --> 01:22:33,150
But every time you
go through a door,

1353
01:22:33,220 --> 01:22:35,950
their plan books will lose
track of you, so it's safer.

1354
01:22:36,330 --> 01:22:39,450
<i>Look, I need you to understand
this has never been done before.</i>

1355
01:22:39,600 --> 01:22:43,000
So, the minute you go through that
first door, all hell will break loose.

1356
01:22:43,070 --> 01:22:45,930
<i>Assume everyone with
a hat on is a threat.</i>

1357
01:22:46,000 --> 01:22:47,400
I don't care if
it's a Yankee cap,

1358
01:22:47,470 --> 01:22:49,270
a bowler, or
even a yarmulke.

1359
01:22:49,340 --> 01:22:52,140
Assume everyone in a hat
is working with Thompson.

1360
01:22:52,280 --> 01:22:54,970
Even you guys can't get through the
doors without your hats on, right?

1361
01:22:55,050 --> 01:22:56,170
Right.

1362
01:22:56,250 --> 01:22:58,650
<i>It's one of the ways the
Chairman limits our power.</i>

1363
01:22:58,720 --> 01:22:59,910
<i>Water is another way.</i>

1364
01:22:59,980 --> 01:23:02,470
And you said before, what if Thompson's
in front of me on Crosby Street?

1365
01:23:02,550 --> 01:23:03,610
Right.

1366
01:23:03,690 --> 01:23:05,590
Why don't I just knock
the hat off his head

1367
01:23:05,660 --> 01:23:08,020
and then run by him before
he has time to pick it up?

1368
01:23:08,090 --> 01:23:10,530
That's good, improvisation.
We have trouble with that.

1369
01:23:11,030 --> 01:23:13,620
The fastest way is actually if
I go right for the blue door.

1370
01:23:13,760 --> 01:23:15,700
<i>Yeah, but then you're
exposed the entire way there.</i>

1371
01:23:15,770 --> 01:23:17,960
That's a lot more dangerous
than leapfrogging through doors.

1372
01:23:18,040 --> 01:23:20,060
But if I make it, I'll take
them completely by surprise.

1373
01:23:20,140 --> 01:23:21,160
If you make it.

1374
01:23:26,510 --> 01:23:29,170
<i>Come on, we have
a lot more to get through.</i>

1375
01:23:29,250 --> 01:23:31,150
Whatever it takes.

1376
01:23:33,680 --> 01:23:36,410
Your father used
to say that.

1377
01:23:43,530 --> 01:23:46,390
I guess you're not supposed
to identify with your subjects.

1378
01:23:46,460 --> 01:23:47,990
You're not supposed
to feel guilt.

1379
01:23:48,770 --> 01:23:51,860
We're not built to lead with
our emotions like you are,

1380
01:23:51,940 --> 01:23:55,390
but that doesn't mean
we don't have them.

1381
01:23:57,140 --> 01:23:58,610
Some more than others.

1382
01:24:21,600 --> 01:24:23,690
You're lucky,
it started raining again.

1383
01:24:23,770 --> 01:24:25,740
We need to get going.
The wedding is in 10 minutes.

1384
01:24:25,800 --> 01:24:29,070
We have time. Being early
is just as bad as being late.

1385
01:24:32,510 --> 01:24:33,670
Is your fiancée here?

1386
01:24:33,740 --> 01:24:35,440
She's on her way in.

1387
01:24:36,410 --> 01:24:38,510
Courtroom 300.

1388
01:24:42,520 --> 01:24:44,510
You okay?
Yeah.

1389
01:24:49,430 --> 01:24:51,020
This is it.
Okay.

1390
01:24:53,500 --> 01:24:55,120
I'm going to go
for the blue door.

1391
01:24:55,200 --> 01:24:56,360
Of course.

1392
01:24:58,500 --> 01:25:01,300
Here is your way through
it. Don't lose it.

1393
01:25:03,310 --> 01:25:04,600
Thanks, Harry.

1394
01:25:04,670 --> 01:25:06,370
Good luck.

1395
01:25:11,850 --> 01:25:13,480
Okay.

1396
01:25:31,400 --> 01:25:34,600
It looks like Mr. Norris is using
the rain to make a run for it.

1397
01:25:34,670 --> 01:25:37,040
Call the proctor
at the wedding.

1398
01:25:39,110 --> 01:25:41,580
What's wrong? Nothing.
I'm just nervous, I guess.

1399
01:25:41,680 --> 01:25:44,610
I just need a minute
in the bathroom, okay?

1400
01:26:06,040 --> 01:26:08,370
Sir, he's got a hat.
He's in the substrate.

1401
01:26:08,440 --> 01:26:10,170
He just went into
the substrate.

1402
01:26:28,230 --> 01:26:29,560
Whoa, whoa.
No, no, no.

1403
01:26:29,630 --> 01:26:31,560
It's an emergency. Sorry.

1404
01:26:31,630 --> 01:26:34,430
Docket number 22,
please come forward.

1405
01:26:34,500 --> 01:26:35,970
We're next. Will you go check on her?

1406
01:26:36,030 --> 01:26:37,360
Yeah.

1407
01:26:44,440 --> 01:26:45,930
You're David Norris!
Yeah, I got to go.

1408
01:26:50,250 --> 01:26:51,340
Nothing in the hat.

1409
01:26:51,410 --> 01:26:52,510
Go ahead.

1410
01:27:08,230 --> 01:27:09,860
She's not even
in there.

1411
01:27:10,570 --> 01:27:13,500
Where is she?
Where is she?

1412
01:27:15,370 --> 01:27:16,930
Elise.

1413
01:27:18,410 --> 01:27:20,430
You're not
marrying that guy.

1414
01:27:23,310 --> 01:27:24,340
I'm so sorry,

1415
01:27:24,410 --> 01:27:27,610
and I don't know what you must
think of me, but you can't marry him.

1416
01:27:27,780 --> 01:27:29,910
You don't love him.
I've been terrible, I know.

1417
01:27:29,990 --> 01:27:31,980
I'm getting married.
No, you can't.

1418
01:27:32,060 --> 01:27:34,320
Don't touch me!
What are you doing?

1419
01:27:34,390 --> 01:27:37,020
Do you know that no one has
ever hurt me as much as you did?

1420
01:27:37,090 --> 01:27:38,820
I'm so sorry that I left
you the way that I did.

1421
01:27:38,900 --> 01:27:41,560
David, you know what? You
left me in a fucking hospital.

1422
01:27:41,630 --> 01:27:42,720
What are you doing?

1423
01:27:42,800 --> 01:27:44,890
I know it seems terrible
but there was a reason.

1424
01:27:44,970 --> 01:27:46,600
You don't know
anything about me

1425
01:27:46,670 --> 01:27:47,860
and what makes me happy.

1426
01:27:47,940 --> 01:27:49,700
I'm sorry that I hurt you.

1427
01:27:51,840 --> 01:27:52,900
Oh, my God.

1428
01:27:52,980 --> 01:27:54,880
Don't worry about him,
he's with them.

1429
01:27:54,950 --> 01:27:56,810
Just listen to me.
Oh, my God, what did you do?

1430
01:27:56,980 --> 01:27:58,740
Forget about him.
What are you doing?

1431
01:27:59,450 --> 01:28:02,820
Look at me, I know
that you love me.

1432
01:28:06,290 --> 01:28:09,820
Okay, this says
that you love me

1433
01:28:09,960 --> 01:28:12,430
and that I love you and that
we're not supposed to be together.

1434
01:28:12,500 --> 01:28:13,660
Because of this book.

1435
01:28:13,730 --> 01:28:15,460
But I know that I'm
supposed to be with you

1436
01:28:15,530 --> 01:28:16,730
because of the way I feel.

1437
01:28:16,800 --> 01:28:18,600
I love you and I don't
care what happens.

1438
01:28:18,670 --> 01:28:21,690
I want to spend the rest of my life with
you even if it's only a little while.

1439
01:28:23,340 --> 01:28:25,310
He just revealed us.

1440
01:28:25,380 --> 01:28:27,000
Call the Intervention Team.

1441
01:28:27,080 --> 01:28:29,510
<i>Tell them it's
a square-one reset.</i>

1442
01:28:35,590 --> 01:28:38,520
Mr. Thompson has deployed
the Intervention Team.

1443
01:28:38,590 --> 01:28:40,890
He's requesting an emergency
Reset Authorization.

1444
01:28:40,990 --> 01:28:42,420
I'll carry it in.

1445
01:28:50,830 --> 01:28:52,430
Why are they chasing you?

1446
01:28:54,840 --> 01:28:56,570
They're going to reset me.

1447
01:28:56,640 --> 01:28:58,130
What are you
talking about?

1448
01:28:59,540 --> 01:29:00,770
I never lied to you.

1449
01:29:00,880 --> 01:29:02,370
Down here.

1450
01:29:02,810 --> 01:29:04,870
He's down here.

1451
01:29:04,980 --> 01:29:06,850
I love you, Elise.

1452
01:29:08,750 --> 01:29:10,720
I'm going to show
you something.

1453
01:29:10,790 --> 01:29:12,850
You deserve to know.

1454
01:29:13,090 --> 01:29:14,680
What's going on?

1455
01:29:23,830 --> 01:29:26,630
We got to keep moving.
There is another door here.

1456
01:29:56,200 --> 01:29:58,830
Got them.
Sixth Avenue.

1457
01:29:59,640 --> 01:30:00,660
Contain this.

1458
01:30:02,810 --> 01:30:05,070
Oh, my God.
What's happening to me?

1459
01:30:05,140 --> 01:30:07,300
All you need to know,
we're being chased.

1460
01:30:07,380 --> 01:30:10,700
I need you to trust me.

1461
01:30:11,210 --> 01:30:12,910
Okay.
Okay, come on.

1462
01:30:23,990 --> 01:30:25,690
Door, door, door!

1463
01:30:28,730 --> 01:30:29,720
Find another door!

1464
01:30:34,200 --> 01:30:36,140
What the hell is going on?

1465
01:30:36,240 --> 01:30:37,900
What the hell is
going on, David?

1466
01:30:37,970 --> 01:30:39,030
Okay, I'm sorry.

1467
01:30:39,110 --> 01:30:40,340
How did we
just do that?

1468
01:30:40,410 --> 01:30:41,880
I'm so sorry.

1469
01:30:41,950 --> 01:30:44,350
These doors belong to the
people who are chasing us.

1470
01:30:44,410 --> 01:30:46,440
Who are they? I don't
know what to call them,

1471
01:30:46,520 --> 01:30:48,110
but they want to take
you away from me.

1472
01:30:48,180 --> 01:30:49,910
Why do they
care about us?

1473
01:30:49,990 --> 01:30:51,540
They hurt your ankle,
Elise.

1474
01:30:51,620 --> 01:30:53,420
Everything you've worked
for your entire life,

1475
01:30:53,490 --> 01:30:55,360
it doesn't happen
if you stay with me.

1476
01:30:55,430 --> 01:30:57,050
That's why I left you
in the hospital.

1477
01:30:57,130 --> 01:30:58,620
I don't understand.

1478
01:30:59,830 --> 01:31:01,760
I don't understand.

1479
01:31:02,800 --> 01:31:04,200
This can't be wrong.

1480
01:31:06,070 --> 01:31:08,200
This can't be wrong.

1481
01:31:09,740 --> 01:31:12,110
Why do they
think it's wrong?

1482
01:31:12,180 --> 01:31:14,510
The book... Their book.

1483
01:31:15,210 --> 01:31:17,080
But what if I can
find who wrote it?

1484
01:31:22,850 --> 01:31:27,150
Okay, I can go
through this door alone.

1485
01:31:27,220 --> 01:31:29,660
You'll never see me or the
people chasing us again.

1486
01:31:32,600 --> 01:31:36,620
Or you could come with me, and I
don't know what's on the other side,

1487
01:31:37,370 --> 01:31:38,660
but I know you
would be next to me,

1488
01:31:38,740 --> 01:31:40,700
and that's all I've wanted
since the minute I met you.

1489
01:31:52,620 --> 01:31:54,210
I'm coming with you.

1490
01:31:58,190 --> 01:31:59,710
Okay.

1491
01:32:02,630 --> 01:32:03,650
Take the other one.

1492
01:32:04,590 --> 01:32:06,190
Turn it to the left.

1493
01:32:06,260 --> 01:32:07,750
To the left.

1494
01:32:17,070 --> 01:32:18,170
Oh, my God.

1495
01:32:37,630 --> 01:32:39,560
Hey!
You can't be in here.

1496
01:32:39,630 --> 01:32:41,100
Stop, sir!

1497
01:32:41,630 --> 01:32:43,100
Hold them up!

1498
01:32:45,270 --> 01:32:46,360
Come on.

1499
01:32:48,740 --> 01:32:50,070
Stop them!

1500
01:33:14,130 --> 01:33:18,260
Harry Mitchell, they want
you in the Chairman's office.

1501
01:33:18,330 --> 01:33:19,860
Immediately.

1502
01:33:39,960 --> 01:33:41,180
Come on.

1503
01:33:54,740 --> 01:33:55,860
Hey! Hey!

1504
01:33:56,240 --> 01:33:58,070
How did you get in here?

1505
01:34:16,330 --> 01:34:17,380
Search the room!

1506
01:34:34,940 --> 01:34:36,170
Shit!

1507
01:34:37,150 --> 01:34:38,370
Go back.

1508
01:35:03,570 --> 01:35:04,670
No.

1509
01:35:05,940 --> 01:35:06,970
Oh, my God.

1510
01:35:13,780 --> 01:35:15,770
I love you.
I love you.

1511
01:36:01,760 --> 01:36:04,160
Did you really think you
could reach the Chairman?

1512
01:36:04,930 --> 01:36:06,700
And change your
fate if you did?

1513
01:36:06,800 --> 01:36:08,930
Or write your own?

1514
01:36:09,240 --> 01:36:12,210
It doesn't work like that
and I told you why.

1515
01:36:14,940 --> 01:36:17,470
I've got a message for you.

1516
01:36:26,920 --> 01:36:28,390
I understand.

1517
01:36:38,300 --> 01:36:40,290
Even Thompson has a boss.

1518
01:36:40,900 --> 01:36:42,060
Harry?

1519
01:36:45,040 --> 01:36:46,070
You're the Chairman?

1520
01:36:46,140 --> 01:36:47,540
No.

1521
01:36:48,240 --> 01:36:50,800
You've met him
though, or her.

1522
01:36:50,880 --> 01:36:52,370
Everybody has.

1523
01:36:53,250 --> 01:36:55,680
The Chairman comes in a
different form to everyone

1524
01:36:55,750 --> 01:36:58,280
so people rarely realize
when it happens.

1525
01:37:01,490 --> 01:37:04,760
Is this some
sort of test?

1526
01:37:04,830 --> 01:37:06,990
In a way, it's all a test,

1527
01:37:07,860 --> 01:37:11,300
for everybody, even the members
of the Adjustment Bureau.

1528
01:37:12,670 --> 01:37:15,300
David, you risked
everything for Elise.

1529
01:37:15,910 --> 01:37:18,810
And Elise, when you came through
that door at the Statue of Liberty,

1530
01:37:18,880 --> 01:37:20,400
you risked everything, too.

1531
01:37:21,440 --> 01:37:22,930
But you inspired me.

1532
01:37:23,550 --> 01:37:25,840
It seems like you
inspired the Chairman, too.

1533
01:37:29,090 --> 01:37:30,450
Is that about us?

1534
01:37:30,520 --> 01:37:31,950
Yes.

1535
01:37:33,760 --> 01:37:34,950
What does it say?

1536
01:37:35,020 --> 01:37:37,250
It says that this situation
between the two of you

1537
01:37:37,330 --> 01:37:39,920
is a serious deviation
from the plan.

1538
01:37:43,330 --> 01:37:45,490
So, the Chairman
rewrote it.

1539
01:37:53,740 --> 01:37:54,840
Okay.

1540
01:37:56,050 --> 01:37:57,710
Now what?

1541
01:37:57,780 --> 01:37:59,110
Now...

1542
01:38:02,820 --> 01:38:04,410
You can take the stairs.

1543
01:38:18,230 --> 01:38:20,700
<i>Most people live life
on the path we set for them.</i>

1544
01:38:21,840 --> 01:38:23,500
<i>Too afraid
to explore any other.</i>

1545
01:38:25,210 --> 01:38:27,770
<i>But once in a while,
people like you come along</i>

1546
01:38:27,840 --> 01:38:31,510
<i>who knock down all the
obstacles we put in your way.</i>

1547
01:38:32,010 --> 01:38:35,810
<i>People who realize free will is a
gift you'll never know how to use</i>

1548
01:38:35,890 --> 01:38:37,790
<i>until you fight for it.</i>

1549
01:38:38,750 --> 01:38:41,380
<i>I think that's the
Chairman's real plan</i>

1550
01:38:41,460 --> 01:38:43,120
<i>that maybe one day</i>

1551
01:38:44,430 --> 01:38:46,550
<i>we won't write the plan.</i>

1552
01:38:47,400 --> 01:38:48,830
<i>You will.</i>

1553
01:38:48,831 --> 01:38:55,837
